ooking at the full breakdown again, the APC 300 STARK is 1296ms total, with constraints taking up 67% of that time—LogUp GKR alone is 40.6%, and I need to find 230ms of savings to hit the 1066ms target.\n\nThe most realistic approach is probably 2-3 more iterations of optimization, each shaving off 50-100ms. For this round, I should focus on the single biggest win, which is LogUp GKR at 526ms. Let me trace through exactly what's happening in that function—it's the challenge setup, the multi-streamed input evaluation, and the GKR protocol rounds, but I need to verify whether the MLE rounds and Round 0 are actually part of this same function or separate.\n\nLooking at the span markers and function calls, prove_zerocheck_and_logup_gpu appears to be the entire Constraints phase, containing the LogUp GKR work plus the other components. Let me confirm the exact boundaries of what's included. I'm trying to trace the actual call hierarchy by looking at the agent reports. It seems like `prove_rap_constraints()` is the top-level function that orchestrates Round 0, the LogUp GKR proof (which includes input evaluations and fractional sumcheck), and then the MLE rounds loop. I need to verify whether the MLE rounds are nested inside `prove_zerocheck_and_logup_gpu()` or called separately at the same level. Looking at the line ranges more carefully, the LogUp GKR phase (lines 404-497) and the MLE rounds loop (lines 656-701) are both nested within the same `prove_zerocheck_and_logup_gpu()` function, with the MLE rounds coming after the GKR work completes. Round 0 appears to be handled somewhere within this same function before the MLE rounds loop begins. I'm seeing the actual execution order now—Round 0 runs first with overlapped logup precompute, then LogUp GKR handles the bus interaction check, and finally MLE Rounds iteratively reduce the sumcheck. The fractional sumcheck is where the real bottleneck sits, consuming about 396ms of the 526ms LogUp GKR time, with roughly 238ms in G