compute-bound and hard to optimize further. So I'm shifting focus to Trace Commit instead, which is 248ms and the second-largest phase. It's got 584 kernel launches for zero hash calculations, and with 623 AIRs there's a lot of independent work that could run concurrently. Multi-streaming has been my most successful strategy, so that's the direction to pursue—though I need to understand the Trace Commit code structure better before diving in.\n\nLooking at the kernel instances more carefully, the 25 poseidon2 launches don't seem to map cleanly to per-commitment work. In STARK proofs, there are typically multiple commitment rounds (main trace, permutation trace, quotient polynomial), and with 2 segments that's around 6 rounds total, which doesn't match what I'm seeing. The Merkle tree construction might be layered differently than I initially thought.\n\nExamining the trace cell distribution across Powdr, Normal Instructions, and OpenVM Precompiles suggests these are committed as separate groups. Each group goes through the same pipeline: stacking AIR traces into a matrix, computing row hashes with Poseidon2, padding hashes, then building the Merkle tree layer by layer. With roughly 10-12 groups per segment across 2 segments, that gives me around 20-24 groups, which aligns much better with the 25 kernel launches I'm seeing.\n\nThe real bottleneck isn't the GPU kernel time itself—that's only about 108ms—but rather the CPU overhead of ~54ms that comes from iterating over all 623 traces per round, constructing metadata, and transferring data to the GPU. Since this happens across 24 rounds over 2 segments, that's roughly 1.15ms of CPU overhead per round. I should focus on pre-computing and caching metadata that stays constant between rounds to reduce this per-round iteration cost.\n\nThat said, even if I save 25-40ms, it's still modest compared to the overall wall time. Let me dig into the H2D transfer data more carefully—there are 30,069 copies totaling 334ms and 3.7GB across all phases, but I need to isolate which ones are happening during the STARK exclusion trace phase specifically. If I can identify and reduce those transfers, the impact could be much larger.\n\nI've been going in circles on analysis though. Let me just commit to the highest-impact idea and start planning the implementation.\n\nLooking at my options, batch trace commit Merkle tree hashing could save 50-100ms if I can make it multi-streamable, though the code structure is uncertain. Pre-computing MLE round metadata is more conservative at 25-40ms but has high confidence and moderate complexity. Overlapping GKR input evaluation with fractional sumcheck across segments could hit 50-80ms but carries significant risk. GPU-accelerated Merkle tree zero hash computation might save 20-40ms if compute-bound, and reducing per-round D2H syncs in fractional sumcheck could shave 10-20ms. There's also potential in pre-computing all monomial evaluation batch structures once rather than repeatedly.\n\nThe key insight is that in the sumcheck_polys_batch_eval function, I'm reconstructing batch builders every round—LogupMonomialBatch, ZerocheckMonomialBatch, and ZerocheckMonomialParYBatch—each doing H2D transfers and kernel launches. Across 5 builders per round, 24 rounds, and 2 segments, that's 240 redundant constructions. The structural parts like block counts and thread decomposition don't actually change between rounds since they depend on fixed parameters like n_y and num_constraints.\n\nSo instead of rebuilding everything each round, I can construct the structure once upfront and then just swap out the data pointers to the current MLE values each iteration. This means building once instead of 240 times, reducing H2D transfers from 3 per builder to just 1 pointer array upload per round, and eliminating the CPU iteration over all AIRs repeatedly. The savings add up to roughly 15ms—about 12ms from avoiding repeated builder construction and another 2.4ms from fewer H2D transfers.\n\nThat's still not huge, but if builder construction is actually slower than my rough estimate, it could matter more. Let me dig into where the real bottleneck is in these MLE rounds.\n\nThe 162ms total spans about 108ms of GPU kernel time and 54ms of CPU overhead. Breaking down that CPU time, the metadata collection for sumcheck_polys_batch_eval is eating up around 30ms—that's the biggest piece. The rest is scattered across descriptor building, polynomial arithmetic, transcript operations, and miscellaneous work. The metadata collection itself involves classifying traces and gathering column pointers for each case, which scales with the number of traces and columns.\n\nIf I can optimize this to only iterate over active traces instead of all traces, the savings compound across rounds. Early rounds have all 623 traces active, but by round 20 I'm down to maybe just 5 traces that still need interpolation, so the per-trace work drops significantly as the protocol progresses.\n\nThe key optimization is recognizing that Case A.2 traces—those already fully folded—don't need individual iteration each round. Their contribution is just a scalar multiplication of their previous values, which I can pre-accumulate once and update cheaply per round. By pre-partitioning traces and batching the Case A.2 work, I reduce the per-round complexity to just handling the shrinking set of Case B traces and the fixed Case A.1 batch.\n\nLooking at the concrete numbers, this drops the average iterations from 623 per round down to roughly 200 across all rounds, with dramatic savings in later rounds where only a handful of Case B traces remain active.\n\nBut even with a 3x reduction in iterations and ~20ms saved per round, that's still pretty modest gains. Let me step back and look for something more substantial—maybe there's a structural bottleneck I'm missing rather than just optimizing the trace collection loop.\n\nThe real issue is that certain components aren't scaling with the APC parameter at all. Round 0 stays flat around 176-181ms, the MLE rounds actually get worse going from 113ms to 162ms, and stacked reduction barely budges. Those are the real drag on reaching the 2x scaling target.\n\nIf I could make all three of those components scale proportionally with the rest of the system, the math works out—I'd actually overshoot the 230ms savings needed. The challenge is figuring out why they're not scaling in the first place and what's causing the MLE rounds to actively degrade at higher APC values.\n\nThe MLE rounds are the biggest culprit here, adding 114ms of excess overhead. The issue is that even though the per-AIR GPU work shrinks, the CPU overhead per AIR stays constant, and with 623 AIRs that compounds quickly. I'm wondering if I could essentially zero out the cost for smaller AIRs by skipping them in the MLE loop entirely and handling them through a different path—though that's basically what Case A.2 attempts, and the iteration overhead itself is still eating into gains.\n\nActually, I should focus on the Trace Commit phase instead. It's sitting at 248ms and hasn't been a major optimization target for the APC 300 case. Let me dig into what's actually happening there in the code.\n\nWait, I need to double-check something about the H2D transfer data I'm looking at.\n\nNow I'm examining the host-to-device transfer metrics. There's 334ms of H2D time across 30,000 copies, which is substantial. These transfers are scattered throughout the benchmark, but a significant chunk likely falls within the STARK exclusion trace phase. If I can trim the H2D overhead in that specific phase, it could move the needle meaningfully. The GKR input evaluation alone suggests 623 AIRs with 2 H2D operations per AIR, which accounts for a chunk of those transfers.\n\nLooking at the breakdown across MLE rounds and Round 0 data uploads, there are many small H2D copies happening. The real bottleneck is that small transfers incur high per-call overhead relative to their