unds would net a win overall.\n\nThat said, this feels like a protocol-level parameter tuning rather than an implementation optimization. Let me refocus on the MLE rounds optimization since that's more concrete. Though I'm also wondering about the `sumcheck_polys_batch_eval` function and whether there's overhead in the monomial evaluation batching that I haven't looked at yet.\n\nLooking at the agent report, I see there are several batch builders being instantiated in that function — one for late logup traces, another for late monomial traces, early trace evaluations, and a couple more for zerocheck. Each constructor allocates vectors and does GPU transfers, and with roughly 240 of these calls happening across all rounds and segments, that's a lot of redundant setup. I'm wondering if caching these batch builders across rounds could eliminate that overhead.\n\nThe key insight is that the structural components — how blocks decompose and which AIR indices map where — only change when AIRs switch between Case B and Case A. The data pointers get updated each round as the fold progresses, but the underlying structure stays the same within a case grouping. So I could group consecutive rounds by their case assignments, build the batch builders once per group, and then just swap out the data pointers for each round. The n_lift values tell me exactly when those transitions happen, so I can sort the AIRs and identify the round boundaries where the case assignment flips.\n\nLooking at the potential savings, I'd go from 240 builder constructions down to maybe 30-40 at the transition points, and the H2D transfers would drop from 720 down to 120-150. That's roughly 10ms from CPU savings and 3ms from H2D, totaling around 13ms — still pretty modest since each constructor only costs about 50 microseconds. This approach isn't going to be the breakthrough I'm looking for.\n\nLet me step back and look at the actual NVTX timeline data to see where the time is really going.","signature":"