needed immediately for the batch polynomial computation, so I can't defer the evaluation sync. The evaluation itself involves GPU kernels that compute the round evaluations, which must complete before the CPU can proceed.\n\nActually, stepping back—I'm seeing 34,444 cudaMemcpyAsync calls totaling 1.3 seconds in the profiler, which is massive. Even if only 40% of that is in the STARK trace, that's still 540ms just on memory transfers. The H2D copies are scattered across multiple places: the GKR input evaluation has thousands of per-AIR data copies, the MLE rounds have hundreds of descriptor arrays, and the batch builders have hundreds of evaluation result copies. Reducing this memory transfer overhead would be the most impactful optimization.\n\nNow I'm weighing different optimization approaches by their expected value—looking at impact, probability of success, and implementation complexity. The trace commit multi-streaming could save 50-100ms but it's complex and uncertain, while precomputing metadata in the MLE rounds might save 25-40ms with higher confidence and lower complexity.\n\nLooking back at what's actually worked before, the biggest wins came from parallelizing specific rounds—like the multistream optimization that saved 313ms on Round 0. Since no single optimization here will get me the full 230ms I need, I'm thinking I need to chain together several medium-sized wins across 3-4 more iterations, targeting 40-80ms per iteration.\n\nThe pattern from successful optimizations is clear: they all tackled obvious structural bottlenecks with fundamental improvements. Now the remaining time is fragmented across many components, which makes this harder. But I'm noticing something—the Trace Commit's Merkle tree hashing hasn't been multi-streamed yet, and that could be a significant opportunity I've overlooked.\n\nLooking at the Trace Commit performance at APC 300, it's at 248ms wall time but has 365ms of GPU work, suggesting there's already some concurrency happenin