erpolation, and monomial batch builder construction with per-trace iteration and vector allocation.\n\nThese O(num_traces) operations per round are the real bottleneck. I'm going to optimize this by pre-classifying traces and caching the invariant batch structures before the MLE loop starts. Since trace classification follows predictable patterns at known round boundaries and the batch builder structures don't change within classification groups, I can compute this metadata once upfront and reuse it across rounds, eliminating most of the per-round CPU work.\n\nThis should save around 25-40ms at APC 300, bringing the time down from 162ms to roughly 122-137ms.\n\nNow I'm shifting focus to a different bottleneck—the Trace Commit phase at 248ms. The row hash kernel takes 44.8ms and there's another 55ms in NTT/blowup operations. I'm wondering if the NTT/blowup could be optimized, or whether multiple commitment rounds for the same segment are running sequentially when they could be parallelized instead.\n\nLooking at how STARK proofs work, each segment goes through a series of sequential steps: committing the main trace, sampling challenges, generating after-challenge traces, committing those, sampling more challenges, running the constraints phase, committing the quotient polynomial, and finally the openings. The challenge sampling creates dependencies between steps, but within a single commitment there might be independent trace groups being processed one after another. At APC 300, the stacked layout combines all traces into one matrix per commitment, so the 44.8ms hash is for one large operation. I'm wondering if I could overlap the NTT/blowup of one commitment with another operation to reduce idle time.\n\nBut then I realize the commitments are interleaved with challenge sampling—commitment 2 can't start until commitment 1's root is finalized and challenges are sampled. Unless there are multiple independent commitments within the same round, pipelining gets tricky. Le