f all preceding kernels. Within each round, I'm launching kernels sequentially for different batch evaluations: interpolation, logup monomial batch, zerocheck monomial batch, logup early batch, zerocheck DAG batch, and zerocheck par_y batch, with to_host() calls after most of them.\n\nSince these kernels are on the same stream, each to_host() call blocks until its preceding kernel finishes, but the kernels themselves execute sequentially rather than in parallel. So the actual synchronization overhead depends on when each to_host() is called relative to kernel completion—if a to_host() is called immediately after a kernel launch, it waits for that kernel to finish before the next kernel can launch.\n\nLooking at the actual numbers from the profiling data, I'm seeing roughly 2.25ms of GPU kernel time per round across all the kernels, plus about 6 to_host() calls at 30μs overhead each, plus CPU-side work like batch construction and trace iteration. That adds up to around 2.8ms per round, which tracks with the measured ~67ms per segment across 24 rounds.\n\nThe remaining gap between the measured 81ms and my calculated 67ms is accounted for by the fold_mle_evals operation — that's another 0.6ms per round from GPU kernels, H2D transfers, and CPU overhead, which totals about 14ms across all rounds. So the full picture is ~54ms of pure GPU computation plus ~27ms of CPU overhead and synchronization costs per segment.\n\nLooking at where that CPU overhead comes from, the to_host sync points are eating up the most time at around 4.3ms per segment, followed by batch builder construction at 3.6ms. The trace iteration, pointer computation, and interpolation overhead are all relatively minor. I should focus on whether those sync points are actually necessary or if there's a way to batch them more efficiently.\n\nThe current approach syncs results back to the host immediately after each batch evaluation so the CPU can write the results into the output arrays. But what if instead I launched all the GPU kernels first, then did a single consolidated sync at the end of the round to pull everything back at once?\n\nDeferring all the host syncs to the end of the round...\n\nThis would let each kernel run asynchronously while the previous ones are still executing, which should improve GPU utilization significantly. The math shows this could save around 150 microseconds per round—reducing from 6 syncs down to 1—which adds up to about 7.2 milliseconds total across all rounds. It's not a huge win, but it's worth considering.\n\nThe real benefit though is that the GPU pipeline stays full instead of going idle during CPU-side processing. Right now the CPU has to wait for results, process them, build the next batch, and launch the next kernel—all while the GPU sits idle. If I defer all the syncs and launch everything upfront, the GPU can keep working continuously. That cuts the wall time per round from about 2.75 milliseconds down to 2.25 milliseconds, since we're no longer adding up all those CPU gaps.\n\nBut there's a constraint I need to think through: the batch builders have to be constructed before launching their kernels, and that construction itself takes time. So even with deferred syncs, the CPU is still building and launching sequentially—it's just not blocking on the GPU results between launches. The GPU processes all the kernels in parallel while the CPU finishes its construction work.\n\nLooking at the actual numbers: building all 6 batch builders takes about 180 microseconds, and the GPU kernels run for roughly 2.25 milliseconds total. Without deferring syncs, I'm paying the sync overhead six times, bringing the total to about 2.43 milliseconds per round. With deferred syncs, I build everything upfront, launch it all, and then do a single sync at the end—that's 2.28 milliseconds per round. So I'm saving about 0.15 milliseconds per round, which compounds to 7.2 milliseconds across all 48 evaluations.\n\nThe problem is that these savings are pretty marginal because the GPU kernels themselves are already quite fast and the sync overhead isn't that expensive to begin with. I'm hitting a wall with MLE round optimizations—the kernel execution time is the real bottleneck, and I can't meaningfully reduce it without either changing the algorithm entirely, doing kernel-level fusions, or trying multi-streaming (which already failed due to memory constraints).\n\nLet me step back and look at the full profile again. LogUp GKR is taking 526 milliseconds, which is over 40% of the total constraint time, so that's probably where I should focus my efforts instead.\n\nLooking at LogUp GKR more closely, the fractional sumcheck dominates at 250-300ms per proof and it's inherently sequential because of the per-round device-to-host transfers and transcript dependencies. The GPU kernels themselves total around 238ms, with the remaining 60-90ms going to overhead. The GKR input evaluation is multi-threaded across 8 streams and runs in about 130ms total, but the actual GPU work is spread across 531ms of kernel time, so each segment only takes about 66ms on the wall clock. I'm wondering if there's something else I could overlap with the GKR input evaluation to hide some of this latency.\n\nThe fractional sumcheck's tree building step needs the leaves from the GKR input evaluation, and those leaves get written to a single device buffer that the tree building kernel reads from. In theory, I could start building the tree as soon as the early streams finish computing their leaves, which might overlap maybe 20ms of tree building with the tail end of input evaluation. But the tree building kernel processes all leaves at once—it can't start until everything is computed. I'd need to restructure it to handle partial leaves, which gets complicated.\n\nFor Trace Commit, the remaining bottleneck is compute-bound hashing, so there's not much optimization potential there. Now looking at the Openings phase, which takes 174ms at APC 300. WHIR dominates at 99ms—it's a polynomial commitment scheme that does algebraic batching of traces followed by multiple folding, hashing, and opening rounds. The profiler shows the algebraic batching kernel taking 18ms across 6 instances, with the folding and moments kernel at 3.3ms, plus merkle tree initialization contributing to the overhead.\n\nThe grind kernel is running 183 instances at 15.7ms total, which is proof-of-work for security adjustments. WHIR scales well at 2.21x, so it's not a scaling bottleneck. The real question is whether I can overlap these phases—instead of running Trace Commit → Constraints → Openings sequentially for each segment, what if I pipeline them so later segments start while earlier ones are still finishing?\n\nBut that won't work because MLE Rounds depend on the constraints from Trace Commit, so they have to be sequential. Looking at this more fundamentally, I need >2x scaling but I'm only getting 1.64x. The path forward is reducing the APC 300 STARK time by about 230ms—the components that already scale well (Trace Commit at 2.13x, WHIR at 2.21x) are pulling their weight, so I need to find what's dragging down the overall ratio.\n\nThe real problem is these three components that barely scale at all: Round 0, MLE Rounds, and Stacked Reduction. They're contributing 416ms at APC 300 when they should only be contributing 155ms if they scaled properly. That's a 261ms gap I need to close.\n\nLooking back at what actually worked—the logup precompute overlap saved 64ms, and the GPU round0 poly extraction saved 47ms—both were about merging phases together rather than optimizing them individually. So I'm thinking about whether I can overlap the after-challenge trace commitment with Round 0 evaluation. The Merkle root from the permutation traces gets committed to the transcript, and Round 0 needs that trace data anyway, so maybe there's a way to pipeline the hashing with the constraint evaluation that follows.\n\nActually, wait—Round 0 also needs the sampled challenges from the transcript, which only exist after the commitment is finalized. So the after-challenge root has to be complete before Round 0 can start. That rules out that overlap.\n\nAnother angle: what if I overlap the openings phase from one segment with the trace commitment of the next segment? They're independent GPU workloads on different data, so they could theoretically run on separate streams. But both are extremely memory-intensive, so running them concurrently might just bottleneck on memory bandwidth instead of actually saving time.\n\nThe math looks promising though—segment 0's openings take about 87ms while segment 1's trace commit takes 124ms, so full overlap could save 87ms. Realistically though, memory contention would probably cut that down to 30-50ms of actual savings. That's still meaningful, but the implementation would be complex since I'd need to restructure the whole segment loop to allow concurrent execution.\n\nI'm also considering whether WHIR itself has parallelization opportunities. It takes 99ms at APC 300, but the structure is inherently sequential—each round depends on the previous one's output, so there's not much room to parallelize there.\n\nNow I'm looking at Round 0's per-AIR overhead more carefully. At APC 300 it's 181ms, which is already 3.65x better than baseline. With 623 AIRs spread across 8 streams, each stream handles about 78 AIRs. The GPU kernels are the main bottleneck—zerocheck and logup evaluation kernels are running thousands of times and consuming most of the 520ms total GPU time in Round 0.\n\nThe measured 181ms wall time versus the expected 130ms from GPU kernel time alone leaves about 51ms of overhead from poly extraction, buffer management, and synchronization. I've already tried batching Round 0 kernels for small AIRs in previous attempts, but ran into Rust optimizer issues causing illegal memory access, and a follow-up workaround also hit problems.\n\nThe batched kernel infrastructure is actually in place, so maybe I could integrate it more carefully this time by avoiding modifications to the sumcheck function that trigger the optimizer. That said, this approach failed twice before and the remaining overhead might not all come from unbatched launches anyway.\n\nAnother angle: what if I overlap the Trace Commit stacking operations with NTT computations to reduce that phase's latency?\n\nLooking at the timing breakdown—stack takes about 2ms, NTT around 5ms, hash 15-45ms depending on size, and compress 1ms—these all run sequentially on the default stream. The challenge is that commitments are interleaved with challenge sampling, so the next commitment can't start until the current one finishes and its root is available. Plus, within a single commitment phase there's typically just one large commitment with all traces stacked together, so there's no real pipelining opportunity there.\n\nI could try multi-streaming the Poseidon2 row hashing alongside NTT, but the hash and NTT kernels operate on the same data and need to stay sequential anyway.\n\nActually, maybe I'm overthinking this. The review pointed me toward either targeting kernel execution directly or looking at a completely different phase. LogUp GKR is the biggest bottleneck at 526ms, so let me focus there instead—it has 41 GKR-related kernels spread across about 80ms of wall time with roughly 14ms of inter-kernel gaps averaging 0.35ms each.\n\nThose gaps come from device-to-host syncs, CPU transcript work, and preparing the next round. They're not massive, but they're worth examining. What's interesting is that the number of GKR rounds itself might be tunable through the `l_skip` parameter—increasing it means pre-computing more of the fraction tree upfront (more GPU work initially) but running fewer interactive rounds afterward, which reduces per-round overhead. The tree building currently takes about 24ms per segment while the sumcheck rounds take around 56ms, so if there are roughly 20 rounds, each one costs about 2.8ms including kernel and overhead.\n\nDoubling `l_skip` to skip two more layers would only save about 0.6ms per segment because the extra tree computation nearly cancels out the round reduction. But there's a more fundamental approach: what if we could run the entire transcript on the GPU instead of bouncing between device and host? If the Poseidon2 sponge executed on GPU, we'd eliminate the D2H sync bottleneck entirely and keep the whole GKR loop running without CPU interaction.\n\nSince Poseidon2 is already implemented for Merkle hashing and the sponge state is tiny, we could structure this as a GPU-side loop that builds trees, computes each round, hashes the result with a small sponge kernel to generate the next challenge, and only copies the final transcript back. This would eliminate roughly 140 D2H transfers and 140 CPU-side transcript operations per proof.\n\nThe real win comes from avoiding inter-kernel gaps—about 28ms per segment. Beyond that, we could potentially use CUDA graphs to capture the entire GKR sequence, but since each round needs different parameters (tree layer, challenge), a static graph won't work. Dynamic parallelism is another option but adds complexity. The cleanest path is probably just running the Poseidon2 sponge on GPU to eliminate the D2H copies and CPU transcript overhead entirely.\n\nLooking at the numbers: we'd save roughly 4ms from eliminating the data transfers, another 1ms from CPU transcript work, and about 10ms from reducing roundtrips—so around 15ms per segment, 30ms total. The implementation requires building a GPU-side sponge state and rewriting the transcript operations as kernels, which is moderately complex but well-defined. Though now I'm second-guessing whether those savings estimates are actually realistic.\n\nLet me dig into the actual profiling data. The nsight traces show about 0.35ms of overhead per kernel transition in the fractional sumcheck—that's 14ms across all 40 inter-kernel gaps per segment