read or warp could handle the post-processing for each round, and then after all GKR rounds finish, I'd do one final D2H copy to bring everything back.\n\nThis would eliminate roughly 40 CPU-GPU roundtrips per segment (20 rounds with 2 transitions each), which is a significant synchronization bottleneck. The implementation would involve creating a GPU-side Poseidon2 sponge and restructuring the round loop to keep everything on-device until completion.\n\nWait, I'm realizing the Poseidon2 sponge is already being used for something else in the pipeline, so I need to think through whether this approach actually works with the existing architecture.\n\nLooking at the CUDA codebase, there's already a sponge implementation and BabyBear Poseidon2 for Merkle trees. The real question is whether I can synchronize the GPU sponge state with the CPU transcript's Fiat-Shamir sponge. If the building blocks are compatible, this becomes a straightforward optimization: initialize the GPU sponge to match the CPU state, run the observe and sample operations on device, then copy the final state back.\n\nBut this is risky — there could be subtle mismatches in field representation or endianness between the GPU and CPU implementations that would break correctness. Given the time I've already spent analyzing, I should probably look for something simpler and more reliable to implement.\n\nLet me step back and evaluate which optimization gives the best return on effort. I'm comparing a few candidates by their impact, success probability, and implementation complexity to find the one with the highest expected value.\n\nLooking at the options, the straightforward ones like caching batch builder structures or deferring to_host() calls have high success rates and simple implementations, but their individual gains are modest. The high-impact ideas are either very complex or have already failed in previous attempts. What if I combined the two simpler approaches — caching structures across rounds while also deferring the to_host() calls — to amplify their effect?\n\nThe challenge is that batch builders change as traces transition between rounds, so I'd need to pre-classify traces by their lift count at the start of the MLE loop and pre-partition them accordingly. But even then, the batch builder construction itself is only about 30 microseconds per builder, and with roughly 5 builders per round, the overhead isn't the real bottleneck here.\n\nLet me shift focus to the Trace Commit phase instead, which is consuming 248ms and represents the second-largest chunk of time. I want to understand what's actually happening in that span—specifically whether the quotient commitment is where the time is going, since the STARK proof involves committing the main trace, computing and committing the permutation trace, evaluating constraints to build the quotient polynomial, and then opening all the committed polynomials. 1, 2, and 3's commitments. But the quotient commitment (step 3) happens AFTER the constraint evaluation.\n\nLooking at the spec.py breakdown:\n```\nSTARK (excl. trace)\n  Constraints (876ms)\n  Openings (174ms)\n  Trace Commit (248ms)\n  Other (3ms)\n```\n\nThe Trace Commit span is SEPARATE from Constraints. In the STARK proof flow:\n- Trace Commit includes: main trace commitment + after-challenge commitment + quotient commitment\n- Constraints includes: Round 0 + GKR + MLE Rounds\n\nBut the quotient polynomial is computed DURING the constraints phase. It's committed AFTER. So the Trace Commit 248ms might be split across different points in the proof.\n\nActually, looking at Plonky3 SWIRL's proof flow:\n1. Commit all traces (multiple commitment rounds for preprocessed + main + derived)\n2. Constraint evaluation + quotient computation\n3. Commit quotient polynomial\n4. Openings\n\nThe \"Trace Commit\" metric might aggregate all commitment operations (both trace and quotient).\n\nNow I'm examining the 25 poseidon2_compressing_row_hashes instances across the tim