h GPU-side transcript processing instead.\n\nThe key insight is that moving the per-round D2H transfers and CPU work in the GKR fractional sumcheck to the GPU could eliminate roughly 40 CPU-GPU roundtrips per segment. Since each roundtrip costs about 0.35ms in inter-kernel overhead, this could save around 28ms total. The main challenge is implementing a GPU sponge kernel that matches the CPU transcript behavior, but the complexity seems manageable compared to the potential speedup.\n\nHowever, I'm realizing the transcript object is more intricate than I initially thought — ensuring byte-level compatibility when moving it to GPU adds real complexity. Let me step back and look at what `observe_and_update` actually does in the GKR code to see if there's a simpler optimization path with comparable impact.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2522,"cache_read_input_tokens":185200,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":2522},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"e33616ae-361c-4ad4-bcb1-e94f2f710e44","uuid":"505ad550-4871-4a35-a576-ac112e8f7429"}
