elf. Looking at the `FiatShamirTranscript` trait, it has two main operations: absorbing extension field elements into a Poseidon2-based sponge and squeezing elements out. The sponge state is typically 16 BabyBear elements, and the operations involve XORing inputs into the state and running permutations. Implementing a Poseidon2 permutation on GPU is straightforward—it's just 12 rounds of S-box operations plus linear mixing.\n\nThe critical part is ensuring the GPU sponge produces identical results to the CPU version, which means matching the permutation parameters, sponge rate/capacity, and the observe/sample protocol exactly. Once that's verified, the approach is to upload the initial sponge state before the GKR rounds, then after each compute round kernel, launch a small sponge kernel that reads the computed values, observes them into the sponge state, squeezes the next challenge, and updates the accumulators—all on device. The final sponge state gets copied back to the host after all rounds complete.\n\nThis is elegant but requires a matching GPU Poseidon2 implementation, restructuring the GKR loop to maintain device-side state, and careful validation that results align. The complexity is moderate with medium risk around sponge mismatches.\n\nLooking at the nsight data more carefully though, the GKR fractional sumcheck shows 14ms of inter-kernel gaps per segment (28ms total), but the measured LogUp GKR is 526ms with only ~329ms of GPU-active time. That leaves ~197ms of overhead, which is much larger than just the inter-kernel gaps. The remaining ~169ms of unaccounted overhead suggests there's something else consuming time—the GKR input evaluation alone takes ~54ms per segment, so I need to trace where the rest is going.\n\nLet me work through the per-segment breakdown more carefully: input eval plus overhead is roughly 64ms, setup is 5-10ms, fractional sumcheck with gaps is around 94ms, and post-sumcheck cleanup is 5ms, which totals about 173ms per segment or 346