rget.\n\nGPU-side transcript processing in GKR fractional sumcheck is more promising—it addresses a real 28ms gap from inter-kernel synchronization, though the implementation is moderately complex and there's some risk around sponge mismatches. But even 28ms is modest, and the risk is real.\n\nActually, I could sidestep the full transcript migration and just eliminate the pipeline drain at the synchronization point. Instead of a blocking cudaMemcpy for the evaluation values, I could use asynchronous memory transfer with CUDA events to let the GPU continue work while the copy happens in the background.\n\nLooking at the actual implementation, `to_host()` on DeviceBuffer does trigger a pipeline drain—even though it's only 32 bytes, the synchronization waits for all preceding GPU work to finish. But here's the thing: by the time we call `to_host()` in the observe_and_update flow, the compute_round kernel has likely already completed, so the drain might be instantaneous anyway.\n\nThe real insight is that the sync time depends on the kernel duration relative to CPU overhead—for quick kernels under 5μs, the CPU work before `to_host()` is enough for completion, but for slower kernels over 1ms, we're actually waiting. That 0.35ms average gap I'm seeing isn't pure pipeline drain; it's a mix of the D2H transfer, CPU work, and kernel prep time.\n\nRather than chase complex optimizations, I should focus on something more practical: deferring the `to_host()` calls so I launch all GPU kernels first, then batch-synchronize them together instead of syncing after each batch evaluation.\n\nLooking at the current flow, there are six separate `to_host()` calls per MLE round—one after logup, one after zerocheck, and so on—each draining the pipeline while the next kernel is only 50-200μs. If I queue all the kernels on the same stream first and then do a single sync at the end, I'd eliminate five redundant pipeline drains and let the GPU work through the full sequence without interruption.\n\nThe math shows each drain costs roughly 20-50μs, so removing five of them saves about 8.4ms across a full batch. On top of that, eliminating the CPU-side gaps between kernel launches—each around 30μs—could add another 7.2ms of GPU utilization gains. That's roughly 15ms total, which is still incremental but meaningful.\n\nThe real win here is architectural: batching all the results before syncing lets the CPU process everything at once instead of context-switching between GPU and CPU repeatedly. Let me think through whether this actually changes the critical path.\n\nLooking at the actual timing breakdown per round, Phase 4 is the bottleneck at 2.2ms—six iterations of building, launching, and pulling results back. With deferred syncing, I'd collapse those six separate to_host calls into one, cutting the phase down to roughly 1.9ms.\n\nThat saves about 0.32ms per round, which scales to around 15ms total across all segments. I keep landing on that same 15ms figure, which is suspiciously close to what we'd gain from rolling back the STARK exclusion.\n\nBut I'm realizing I might be underestimating the sync cost—to_host doesn't just copy memory, it also stalls until the GPU kernel actually finishes. For the smaller late eval kernels, that could mean waiting for work that's still in flight, not just the memcpy overhead.\n\nLooking at the deferral strategy: if I batch all six kernels to run back-to-back, they'd take about 1.5ms total, then a single to_host call would wait for the last kernel to finish. Without deferral, each kernel-sync pair adds up differently depending on whether the CPU has time to prepare the next batch before the GPU finishes—the first kernel might have 170μs of execution still pending when to_host is called, creating variable drain times across the sequence.\n\nThe real win is that deferral eliminates five redundant synchronization points where the CPU would otherwise idle waiting for each kernel. Those five extra drains cost roughly 850μs total, whereas deferral only needs one final drain of about 170μs. That's a savings of roughly 680μs per round per segment, which scales to about 32ms across all segments and rounds—a much more substantial improvement than the initial latency analysis suggested. I'm seeing that my wall time calculation was off—let me reconsider how the timing actually works. Without deferral, each kernel launch blocks until the previous one completes, so we're stuck at 2520μs total. But with deferral, I can batch all the builds and launches upfront while the GPU processes them in parallel, then do a single synchronization and batch process. That brings the wall time down to around 1490μs, which is a significant improvement since the GPU work overlaps with the CPU build phase. With deferral, I can queue all six kernel builds on the CPU side first—taking 180μs total—while the GPU processes them sequentially. The GPU runs for 1200μs straight through all kernels, then the CPU does a single batch transfer back, waiting for the remaining 1020μs of GPU work plus 30μs for the memcpy operations, bringing the total to around 1050μs after the builds complete.\n\nThe key difference is that without deferral, the GPU sits idle for 250μs total across five separate sync points while the CPU builds and processes each batch, but with deferral the GPU never goes idle—it's continuously executing kernels while the CPU gets ahead on the next batch of builds. I'm realizing the savings are actually quite minimal—only around 4.8ms total—because the CPU is already the limiting factor at 1350μs, so reducing GPU idle time doesn't meaningfully improve wall time. Let me retrace the execution more carefully, since I think I was double-counting the sequential CPU and GPU operations. With deferred execution, I can overlap the build phases with kernel launches—building the next iteration while the GPU processes the current one. This compresses the timeline significantly: all six kernels launch by t=180, then I wait for the final one to complete at t=1230, followed by a single memcpy and processing phase. The total round time drops to around 1500μs, but the key win is that the CPU stays busy building while the GPU runs kernels in parallel.\n\nHowever, the savings of roughly 5.8ms per segment across all rounds still falls short of the rollback threshold. The real issue is that the GPU kernels themselves—each taking 200μs—are the dominant cost, far outweighing the CPU overhead of building and processing. Even with perfect overlap, I'm limited by the kernel execution time, not the CPU scheduling gaps.\n\nI've been cycling through different optimization angles without making real progress. Let me commit to the GPU-side transcript approach for the GKR fractional sumcheck instead. It directly targets the largest bottleneck at 526ms and could yield around 28ms in savings, which would be meaningful. Even if the actual gains end up smaller than projected, it's a fresh strategy worth exploring.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1766,"cache_read_input_tokens":203549,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1766},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"e33616ae-361c-4ad4-bcb1-e94f2f710e44","uuid":"b30b7116-2bf2-4d2f-af39-eee422cd6d0d"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01BqBHmhgGeXJZejwc1W1LqE","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"The reviewer is correct — the prior task `2026-04-13-1430-batch-mle-main-ptrs-upload` attempted this exact optimization and measured only 5ms improvement. I need to select a different target.\n\nAfter revisiting the analysis, I'll target the **LogUp GKR fractional sumcheck per-round CPU-GPU roundtrip overhead** by moving the per-round transcript processing to GPU. This is a fundamentally different approach from anything tried before.\n\nLet me update the task and plan."}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1766,"cache_read_input_tokens":203549,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1766},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"e33616ae-361c-4ad4-bcb1-e94f2f710e44","uuid":"598cb306-bca5-4f89-a764-1fd29bc2a699"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01BqBHmhgGeXJZejwc1W1LqE","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01XZzAjjkqYDSZrZchbSAkTP","name":"Write","input":{"file_path":"/home/georg/stark-backend/autoopt-results/task.md","content":"# 2026-04-14-1800-gpu-gkr-transcript-processing\n\nEliminate per-round CPU-GPU roundtrips in the GKR fractional sumcheck by moving the transcript observe/sample operations to GPU. Currently, each GKR sumcheck round launches a GPU kernel, then does a blocking D2H copy of 2 field elements, CPU-side arithmetic (reconstruct_s_evals), CPU-side Poseidon2 transcript absorb/squeeze, and finally launches the next round's kernel. With ~20 rounds per segment × 2 segments = ~40 roundtrips, nsight profiling shows 14ms of inter-kernel gap per segment (28ms total) from these CPU-GPU transitions. By implementing a small GPU kernel that fuses the post-round processing (reconstruct, sponge observe, sponge squeeze, accumulator update), we eliminate all mid-loop D2H copies and keep the entire GKR round loop on the GPU, with a single D2H at the end to retrieve the final transcript state.\n"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1766,"cache_read_input_tokens":203549,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1766},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"e33616ae-361c-4ad4-bcb1-e94f2f710e44","uuid":"e862b5c3-e743-472b-82bd-ce80f4bd9043"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01XZzAjjkqYDSZrZchbSAkTP","type":"tool_result","content":"The file /home/georg/stark-backend/autoopt-results/task.md has been updated successfully."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"e33616ae-361c-4ad4-bcb1-e94f2f710e44","uuid":"d113f848-78a8-4b9e-bd86-6dc6009431d4","timestamp":"2026-04-14T16:23:31.296Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"update","filePath":"/home/georg/stark-backend/autoopt-results/task.md","content":"# 2026-04-14-1800-gpu-gkr-transcript-processing\n\nEliminate per-round CPU-GPU roundtrips in the GKR fractional sumcheck by moving the transcript observe/sample operations to GPU. Currently, each GKR sumcheck round launches a GPU kernel, then does a blocking D2H copy of 2 field elements, CPU-side arithmetic (reconstruct_s_evals), CPU-side Poseidon2 transcript absorb/squeeze, and finally launches the next round's kernel. With ~20 rounds per segment × 2 segments = ~40 roundtrips, nsight profiling shows 14ms of inter-kernel gap per segment (28ms total) from these CPU-GPU transitions. By implementing a small GPU kernel that fuses the post-round processing (reconstruct, sponge observe, sponge squeeze, accumulator update), we eliminate all mid-loop D2H copies and keep the entire GKR round loop on the GPU, with a single D2H at the end to retrieve the final transcript state.\n","structuredPatch":[{"oldStart":1,"oldLines":3,"newStart":1,"newLines":3,"lines":["-# 2026-04-14-1800-batch-mle-per-trace-h2d","+# 2026-04-14-1800-gpu-gkr-transcript-processing"," ","-Replace per-trace `main_ptrs.to_device()` calls in MLE sumcheck round evaluation with per-round bulk H2D uploads. Currently, each MLE round builds a `Vec<MainMatrixPtrs>` for every Case B trace and uploads it individually, generating ~5000 H2D copies per segment (~10K total at APC 300). nsight profiling confirms the CUDA API overhead for these small transfers (~5μs each) consumes ~25ms per segment. By concatenating all per-trace MainMatrixPtrs into a single Vec and performing one bulk `to_device()` per round, we reduce ~10K H2D copies to ~48 (24 rounds × 2 segments), saving an estimated 40-50ms on MLE Rounds at APC 300.","+Eliminate per-round CPU-GPU roundtrips in the GKR fractional sumcheck by moving the transcript observe/sample operations to GPU. Currently, each GKR sumcheck round launches a GPU kernel, then does a blocking D2H copy of 2 field elements, CPU-side arithmetic (reconstruct_s_evals), CPU-side Poseidon2 transcript absorb/squeeze, and finally launches the next round's kernel. With ~20 rounds per segment × 2 segments = ~40 roundtrips, nsight profiling shows 14ms of inter-kernel gap per segment (28ms total) from these CPU-GPU transitions. By implementing a small GPU kernel that fuses the post-round processing (reconstruct, sponge observe, sponge squeeze, accumulator update), we eliminate all mid-loop D2H copies and keep the entire GKR round loop on the GPU, with a single D2H at the end to retrieve the final transcript state."]}],"originalFile":"# 2026-04-14-1800-batch-mle-per-trace-h2d\n\nReplace per-trace `main_ptrs.to_device()` calls in MLE sumcheck round evaluation with per-round bulk H2D uploads. Currently, each MLE round builds a `Vec<MainMatrixPtrs>` for every Case B trace and uploads it individually, generating ~5000 H2D copies per segment (~10K total at APC 300). nsight profiling confirms the CUDA API overhead for these small transfers (~5μs each) consumes ~25ms per segment. By concatenating all per-trace MainMatrixPtrs into a single Vec and performing one bulk `to_device()` per round, we reduce ~10K H2D copies to ~48 (24 rounds × 2 segments), saving an estimated 40-50ms on MLE Rounds at APC 300.\n"}}
