anging the kernel signature so `lambda` and `prev_r` come from device memory rather than as scalar arguments, but the performance hit from pointer indirection is negligible for a kernel that already runs for 500+ microseconds.\n\nI'm going to go with option 3 (a separate small kernel) for now since it's simpler to implement, and I can always fuse it later as an optimization.\n\nActually, let me reconsider the inner round count. I calculated 844 per segment based on dividing 1687 total compute_round instances by 2 segments, but I need to account for the recursion proofs mixed in. Looking at the nsight data, I see compute_round_and_revert, compute_round_and_fold_inplace, and compute_round_and_fold kernels spread across the app proof segments plus the leaf, inner, and compression recursion proofs. For just the app proof segments, the tree building shows 15 layers per segment, which means roughly 15 GKR outer rounds per segment. Since the inner rounds scale with the outer round index, the total is the sum from 1 to 15.\n\nThat gives me 120 inner rounds per segment, or 240 for both app proof segments. But the remaining 1447 compute_round instances seem too high for just the recursion proofs, so my estimate of 15 outer rounds might be off. The tree building kernels suggest closer to 30 layers when accounting for the two-layer fusion and the initial bit reversal kernel, which would change the outer round count significantly.\n\nLooking at the actual kernel breakdown from the data: one bit reversal kernel handles the first two layers, thirteen two-layer fusion kernels cover the next 26 layers, and a final single-layer kernel handles layer 28, totaling 29 layers in the tree.\n\nWith 29 outer rounds, the inner rounds sum to 406 per segment, or 812 across both segments for the app proof. The nsight data shows 1687 total compute_round instances, so the remaining 875 likely correspond to the recursion proofs. This means the app proof overhead is roughly 406 inner rounds × 24μs per segment, or about 9.7ms per segment and 19.5ms total—lower than my earlier estimate because I was incorrectly including recursion kernel instances in the calculation.\n\nLooking back at the nsight gap analysis, I realize I only captured a subset of kernels: compute_round_and_revert, multifold, and precompute_m_build_partial. I completely missed the main inner round kernel, compute_round_and_fold_inplace, which accounts for the majority of instances, so I need to redo the analysis with all kernel types included.\n\nIf segment 0 has around 406 inner rounds and my gap measurements covered only 41 kernels with 14.2ms of gaps, the full picture would include roughly 450 total kernels. The compute_round_and_fold_inplace kernels are extremely small at 6.4μs average, so their inter-kernel gaps are likely dominated by launch overhead rather than D2H synchronization, especially since observe_and_update only triggers after certain kernels, not all of them.\n\nLooking more carefully at the actual code structure: the PrecomputeM strategy batches multiple inner rounds into a single kernel call, while FoldEval launches one kernel per inner round. The key insight is that observe_and_update happens once per outer round after all inner rounds complete, not once per inner round. I need to trace through the PrecomputeM strategy more carefully to understand whether observe_and_update is called once per inner round or once for all rounds combined, since that directly impacts the device-to-host overhead.\n\nLooking at the nsight profiler data, I'm seeing roughly 14ms of inter-kernel gaps per segment during the fractional sumcheck, which accounts for all the per-round overhead including device-to-host transfers and CPU transcript updates. Moving this work to the GPU could eliminate most of that 14ms per segment, saving around 28ms total across both segments.\n\nI've been analyzing this for a while now, so let me just focus on writing up the GPU-side transcript optimization plan—even if the gains are modest, it's a solid architectural improvement worth pursuing.\n\nActually, before I commit to that approach, I should recognize that the GKR round loop uses multiple strategies like FoldEval and PrecomputeM, each calling observe_and_update from different code paths with varying GPU kernel output formats. Moving the entire transcript logic to GPU would require coordinating changes across all these paths, which adds significant complexity. A simpler alternative might be to just make the device-to-host copy asynchronous so it overlaps with the next round's EqLayers computation on the CPU side.\n\nThe challenge is that SqrtEqLayers construction happens at the start of each outer round before any inner rounds begin, and both inner and outer rounds execute sequentially. So I could potentially overlap the last inner round's asynchronous D2H transfer with the next outer round's EqLayers construction, letting the CPU work proceed while the transfer completes in the background, then synchronizing before the transcript operations.\n\nHowever, the D2H payload is only 32 bytes—essentially instantaneous—and the EqLayers construction itself is also quite fast, so the overlap window is negligible. Given this constraint, I'm going to move forward with the GPU-side transcript approach instead. Now I need to map out the specific implementation: a new CUDA kernel that handles the postprocessing work (reconstructing evaluations, feeding the sponge, sampling challenges, and updating accumulators), modifications to the GKR round loop in fractional.rs to invoke this kernel instead of the CPU observe_and_update call, and device-side buffers for the sponge state, round challenges, round polynomial evaluations, and EQ values.\n\nSetting up device buffers for the accumulators and previous evaluations, then after the loop completes, copying everything back to the host and syncing the CPU transcript.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usa