{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01Hy2y7cWzk4Ai7HzWqtPdvD","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# Plan: GPU-Side GKR Transcript Processing\n2\t\n3\t## Goal\n4\t\n5\tEliminate per-round CPU-GPU roundtrips in the GKR fractional sumcheck by moving the `observe_and_update` function (D2H copy + arithmetic + Poseidon2 transcript observe/sample) to a GPU kernel. This targets the 14ms per-segment inter-kernel gap measured by nsight in the fractional sumcheck round loop (28ms total across 2 segments at APC 300).\n6\t\n7\t## Current Code Path\n8\t\n9\t### Call chain\n10\t\n11\t```\n12\tfractional_sumcheck_gpu() @ fractional.rs:491\n13\t└─ for round in 1..total_rounds (line 655)\n14\t    ├─ eq_buffer = SqrtEqLayers::from_xi() (line 663) — CPU\n15\t    ├─ lambda = transcript.sample_ext() (line 670) — CPU Poseidon2 sponge\n16\t    ├─ do_sumcheck_round_and_revert() (line 698) — GPU kernel + observe_and_update\n17\t    └─ match backend:\n18\t         ├─ FoldEval: for xi_j { do_fused_sumcheck_round() } — GPU + observe_and_update per inner round\n19\t         └─ PrecomputeM: precompute_m → multifold → observe_and_update (fewer calls)\n20\t```\n21\t\n22\t### Per-round observe_and_update (fractional.rs:318-346)\n23\t\n24\tEach call does:\n25\t1. **D2H sync**: `d_sum_evals.to_host()` — copies 2 EF (32 bytes) from GPU, BLOCKS until preceding GPU kernel completes\n26\t2. **CPU arithmetic**: `reconstruct_s_evals()` — ~12 EF field operations (inverse, interpolation)\n27\t3. **CPU transcript**: `transcript.observe_ext()` × 3 — absorbs 12 BabyBear elements, triggers 1-2 Poseidon2 permutations\n28\t4. **CPU transcript**: `transcript.sample_ext()` — squeezes 4 BabyBear elements, triggers 0-1 Poseidon2 permutations\n29\t5. **CPU accumulator update**: `eq_r_acc *= ...`, `prev_s_eval = ...`\n30\t6. **Return**: challenge `r` used by next round's GPU kernel (passed as scalar argument)\n31\t\n32\t### Why it's slow\n33\t\n34\tnsight profiling of the fractional sumcheck kernel timeline shows:\n35\t- Segment 0: 80.5ms wall time, 14.2ms of inter-kernel gaps across ~41 major kernel transitions (mean gap: 0.35ms)\n36\t- Segment 1: 80.3ms wall time, 14.0ms of inter-kernel gaps\n37\t\n38\tEach gap is a CPU-GPU roundtrip: the GPU idles while the CPU does D2H + reconstruct + transcript + launch next kernel. The GPU compute kernels themselves (compute_round_and_revert, compute_round_and_fold_inplace, multifold, precompute_m) total only ~119ms GPU time per segment.\n39\t\n40\t### Existing GPU infrastructure\n41\t\n42\tThe codebase already has:\n43\t- **GPU Poseidon2 permutation**: `poseidon2_mix(Fp *cells)` in `crates/cuda-common/include/poseidon2.cuh:184-202`\n44\t- **GPU sponge state**: `DeviceSpongeState { Fp state[16]; uint32_t absorb_idx; uint32_t sample_idx; }` in `poseidon2.cuh`\n45\t- **GPU sponge operations**: `sponge_observe(state, val)` and `sponge_sample(state)` device functions in `poseidon2.cuh:24-42`\n46\t- **Host-device sponge sync**: `DuplexSpongeGpu` in `crates/cuda-backend/src/sponge.rs` already synchronizes sponge state between host and device (used by `grind_gpu`)\n47\t\n48\t## Changes\n49\t\n50\t### Change 1: New CUDA kernel `gkr_round_postprocess`\n51\t\n52\t**File**: `crates/cuda-backend/cuda/src/fractional_sumcheck.cu` (new file, or append to existing sumcheck.cu)\n53\t\n54\tCreate a single-thread GPU kernel that replaces `observe_and_update`:\n55\t\n56\t```cuda\n57\t__global__ void gkr_round_postprocess(\n58\t    const FpExt *d_sum_evals,      // 2 EF values from compute_round kernel\n59\t    DeviceSpongeState *sponge,     // transcript sponge state (modified in-place)\n60\t    FpExt *d_prev_s_eval,          // scalar, modified in-place\n61\t    FpExt *d_eq_r_acc,             // scalar, modified in-place\n62\t    FpExt xi_j,                    // constant per call\n63\t    FpExt *d_challenge_out,        // output: sampled challenge r\n64\t    FpExt *d_s_evals_out,          // output: s_evals[0..3] for round_polys_eval\n65\t    FpExt *d_sp_evals_out          // output: sp_evals for debugging/verification (optional)\n66\t) {\n67\t    // 1. Reconstruct s_evals (same logic as fractional.rs:1126-1163)\n68\t    FpExt eq_r_acc = *d_eq_r_acc;\n69\t    FpExt prev_s_eval = *d_prev_s_eval;\n70\t    \n71\t    FpExt sp_evals[3];\n72\t    sp_evals[1] = d_sum_evals[0] * eq_r_acc;\n73\t    sp_evals[2] = d_sum_evals[1] * eq_r_acc;\n74\t    FpExt eq_xi_0 = FpExt::one() - xi_j;\n75\t    FpExt eq_xi_1 = xi_j;\n76\t    sp_evals[0] = (prev_s_eval - eq_xi_1 * sp_evals[1]) * inverse(eq_xi_0);\n77\t    \n78\t    // Interpolate at X=3 (quadratic through 0,1,2)\n79\t    // sp(3) = sp(0) - 3*sp(1) + 3*sp(2) (Newton form for quadratic)\n80\t    // Actually: Lagrange at points 0,1,2 evaluated at 3\n81\t    \n82\t    // Compute s_evals[i] = eq(xi_j, i+1) * sp(i+1)\n83\t    FpExt s_evals[3];\n84\t    for (int i = 0; i < 3; i++) {\n85\t        FpExt x = FpExt::from_u32(i + 1);\n86\t        FpExt sp_x = (i < 2) ? sp_evals[i + 1] : interpolate_quadratic_012(sp_evals, x);\n87\t        s_evals[i] = eval_eq_mle_1(xi_j, x) * sp_x;\n88\t    }\n89\t    d_s_evals_out[0] = s_evals[0];\n90\t    d_s_evals_out[1] = s_evals[1];\n91\t    d_s_evals_out[2] = s_evals[2];\n92\t    \n93\t    // 2. Transcript observe: 3 EF = 12 F\n94\t    for (int i = 0; i < 3; i++) {\n95\t        // observe_ext: absorb 4 base field coefficients\n96\t        for (int c = 0; c < 4; c++) {\n97\t            sponge_observe(*sponge, s_evals[i].coeffs[c]);\n98\t        }\n99\t    }\n100\t    \n101\t    // 3. Transcript sample: 1 EF = 4 F\n102\t    FpExt r;\n103\t    for (int c = 0; c < 4; c++) {\n104\t        r.coeffs[c] = sponge_sample(*sponge);\n105\t    }\n106\t    *d_challenge_out = r;\n107\t    \n108\t    // 4. Update accumulators\n109\t    FpExt eq_r = eval_eq_mle_1(xi_j, r);\n110\t    *d_eq_r_acc = eq_r_acc * eq_r;\n111\t    *d_prev_s_eval = eq_r * interpolate_quadratic_012(sp_evals, r);\n112\t}\n113\t```\n114\t\n115\tThis kernel runs on 1 thread (<<<1,1>>>). It needs:\n116\t- `FpExt` (BinomialExtensionField<BabyBear, 4>) arithmetic in CUDA: add, sub, mul, inverse\n117\t- `eval_eq_mle_1(xi, x)` = `xi * x + (1-xi) * (1-x)` — trivial\n118\t- `interpolate_quadratic_012(vals, x)` — Lagrange interpolation of degree-2 poly through points (0,1,2) evaluated at x\n119\t- Existing `sponge_observe` and `sponge_sample` from poseidon2.cuh\n120\t\n121\t### Change 2: EF arithmetic in CUDA\n122\t\n123\t**File**: `crates/cuda-common/include/ext_field.cuh` (new or extend existing)\n124\t\n125\tIf EF (BinomialExtensionField<BabyBear,4>) arithmetic doesn't already exist in CUDA device code, implement:\n126\t- `FpExt` struct: 4 `Fp` coefficients\n127\t- Add, sub, mul (using the binomial extension multiplication formula)\n128\t- Inverse (using extended Euclidean or Fermat's little theorem)\n129\t- `from_u32`, `one()`, `zero()`\n130\t\n131\tCheck first: the GKR input evaluation kernel already uses `FracExt` which contains `FpExt` members. The extension field arithmetic likely exists in the codebase.\n132\t\n133\t### Change 3: FFI wrapper for gkr_round_postprocess\n134\t\n135\t**File**: `crates/cuda-backend/src/cuda/fractional.rs` (new, or extend existing cuda module)\n136\t\n137\tAdd Rust FFI binding:\n138\t```rust\n139\textern \"C\" {\n140\t    fn _gkr_round_postprocess(\n141\t        d_sum_evals: *const EF,\n142\t        sponge: *mut DeviceSpongeState,\n143\t        d_prev_s_eval: *mut EF,\n144\t        d_eq_r_acc: *mut EF,\n145\t        xi_j: EF,  // passed by value (16 bytes)\n146\t        d_challenge_out: *mut EF,\n147\t        d_s_evals_out: *mut EF,\n148\t        d_sp_evals_out: *mut EF,\n149\t    ) -> i32;\n150\t}\n151\t```\n152\t\n153\t### Change 4: Modify fractional sumcheck round loop\n154\t\n155\t**File**: `crates/cuda-backend/src/logup_zerocheck/fractional.rs`\n156\t\n157\tBefore the GKR round loop (line 655):\n158\t1. **Upload sponge state**: Sync the current CPU transcript state to a `DeviceBuffer<DeviceSpongeState>` (the existing `DuplexSpongeGpu::sync_h2d()` pattern)\n159\t2. **Allocate device buffers**: `d_prev_s_eval`, `d_eq_r_acc`, `d_challenges` (array of total_rounds EF), `d_round_polys_eval` (array of total_rounds × 3 EF), `d_sp_evals_scratch`\n160\t\n161\tReplace `observe_and_update()` calls (lines 382, and within do_fused_sumcheck_round and PrecomputeM paths) with:\n162\t```rust\n163\tunsafe { gkr_round_postprocess(\n164\t    d_sum_evals.as_ptr(),\n165\t    d_sponge.as_mut_ptr(),\n166\t    d_prev_s_eval.as_mut_ptr(),\n167\t    d_eq_r_acc.as_mut_ptr(),\n168\t    xi_j,\n169\t    d_challenges.as_mut_ptr().add(challenge_idx),\n170\t    d_round_polys_ptr.add(round_polys_idx * 3),\n171\t    d_sp_evals_scratch.as_mut_ptr(),\n172\t)?; }\n173\t```\n174\t\n175\tModify the next round's compute kernel to read the challenge from `d_challenges[challenge_idx]` instead of receiving it as a scalar argument. The challenge is already on device — pass a device pointer.\n176\t\n177\t### Change 5: Post-loop D2H and CPU state synchronization\n178\t\n179\t**File**: `crates/cuda-backend/src/logup_zerocheck/fractional.rs`\n180\t\n181\tAfter the GKR round loop completes:\n182\t1. **D2H**: Copy `d_round_polys_eval`, `d_challenges` (r_vec), `d_eq_r_acc`, `d_prev_s_eval`, `d_sponge` back to host\n183\t2. **Update CPU transcript**: Restore the CPU transcript's sponge state from the downloaded GPU sponge state (reverse of sync_h2d)\n184\t3. **Populate CPU data structures**: Fill `round_polys_eval` and `r_vec` from the D2H'd arrays\n185\t4. **Continue**: The rest of the proof uses the CPU transcript as before\n186\t\n187\t### Change 6: Pass challenge by device pointer to compute_round kernels\n188\t\n189\t**Files**: CUDA kernel wrappers in `crates/cuda-backend/src/cuda/logup_zerocheck.rs`, CUDA kernels in `crates/cuda-backend/cuda/src/sumcheck.cu`\n190\t\n191\tModify `frac_compute_round_and_fold`, `frac_compute_round_and_revert`, etc. to accept the previous round's challenge `r` via device pointer instead of scalar value. Add a `__device__` read at kernel start:\n192\t```cuda\n193\tFpExt r = *d_r_ptr;  // read from device memory (16 bytes, L1 cached)\n194\t```\n195\t\n196\tThis is a minimal change to the existing kernels. The pointer argument replaces the scalar argument. Read latency is negligible (L1 cache hit).\n197\t\n198\t## Invariants\n199\t\n200\t1. **Transcript determinism**: The GPU sponge must produce identical observe/sample sequences as the CPU sponge. Both use the same Poseidon2 permutation (verified: constants in poseidon2.cuh match the CPU implementation), the same state layout (WIDTH=16, RATE=8), and the same duplex mode (overwrite-absorb). A verification test should compare GPU-produced challenges against CPU-produced challenges for the same input.\n201\t\n202\t2. **No protocol change**: The cryptographic protocol is unchanged. The same challenges are produced in the same order. Only the execution location (CPU → GPU) changes.\n203\t\n204\t3. **Correctness of eq_r_acc and prev_s_eval**: These accumulators are used by subsequent rounds. The GPU arithmetic must exactly match the CPU EF arithmetic. BabyBear field arithmetic is deterministic (modular arithmetic mod p = 2^27 * 15 + 1).\n205\t\n206\t4. **No APC 0 regression**: At APC 0 (99 AIRs, 5 segments), the GKR has fewer rounds per segment. The overhead from initializing device buffers and the final D2H must not exceed the savings. The optimization should be neutral-to-positive at APC 0 since there are still per-round roundtrips to eliminate.\n207\t\n208\t5. **Sponge state consistency**: After the GKR round loop, the CPU transcript must be updated with the GPU's final sponge state before ANY subsequent transcript operations. This is critical for Fiat-Shamir security.\n209\t\n210\t## Measurement Plan\n211\t\n212\t1. Build and run the standard benchmarks:\n213\t```bash\n214\tcd /home/georg/powdr/results/pairing\n215\tcargo build --bin powdr_openvm_riscv -r --features \"metrics,cuda\"\n216\tPROVE_BIN=<path>/release/powdr_openvm_riscv\n217\t$PROVE_BIN prove --artifact apc300.cbor --input 0 --metrics <path>/after_apc300.json --recursion\n218\t$PROVE_BIN prove --artifact apc000.cbor --input 0 --metrics <path>/after_apc000.json --recursion\n219\t```\n220\t\n221\t2. Analyze with `python3 /home/georg/spec.py <metrics_path> <name>`\n222\t\n223\t3. Run nsight profiling for APC 300 to verify inter-kernel gap reduction:\n224\t```bash\n225\tnsys profile --output <output> --force-overwrite true --trace cuda,nvtx,osrt --sample none --stats true -- $PROVE_BIN prove --artifact apc300.cbor --input 0 --recursion\n226\t```\n227\t\n228\t4. Verify: query nsight sqlite for fractional sumcheck kernel gaps (same analysis as pre-optimization baseline).\n229\t\n230\t### Expected results\n231\t- LogUp GKR at APC 300: 526ms → ~498ms (28ms reduction from eliminated roundtrips)\n232\t- STARK excl trace at APC 300: 1296ms → ~1268ms\n233\t- Fractional sumcheck inter-kernel gaps: 14ms → ~2ms per segment (mostly kernel launch overhead remaining)\n234\t- No regression at APC 0\n235\t\n236\t### Verification\n237\t- Compare GPU-produced challenges against a reference CPU run for the same proof. All challenges must match exactly.\n238\t- Proof verification must pass at APC 0, 100, and 300.\n239\t\n240\t## Rollback Criteria\n241\t\n242\tRevert if:\n243\t- LogUp GKR improvement at APC 300 is less than 10ms\n244\t- Any regression at APC 0 exceeds 10ms on STARK excl trace\n245\t- Proof verification fails at any APC configuration\n246\t- GPU-produced challenges differ from CPU reference (indicates sponge mismatch)\n247\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01Bk2X7Ug1SRvaG861UVxTFP","session_id":"e33616ae-361c-4ad4-bcb1-e94f2f710e44","uuid":"347cf302-f1ac-467a-82e7-f117e75ab6ee","timestamp":"2026-04-14T16:29:01.275Z"}
