{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01DQgpbZCHN8AfCB6jKSgcfg","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01VDhLMbzgkzDXMq8HEhEPAN","name":"Write","input":{"file_path":"/home/georg/stark-backend/autoopt-results/2026-04-14-1800-gpu-gkr-transcript-processing/plan.md","content":"# Plan: GPU-Side GKR Transcript Processing\n\n## Goal\n\nEliminate per-round CPU-GPU roundtrips in the GKR fractional sumcheck by moving the `observe_and_update` function (D2H copy + arithmetic + Poseidon2 transcript observe/sample) to a GPU kernel. This targets the 14ms per-segment inter-kernel gap measured by nsight in the fractional sumcheck round loop (28ms total across 2 segments at APC 300).\n\n## Current Code Path\n\n### Call chain\n\n```\nfractional_sumcheck_gpu() @ fractional.rs:491\n└─ for round in 1..total_rounds (line 655)\n    ├─ eq_buffer = SqrtEqLayers::from_xi() (line 663) — CPU\n    ├─ lambda = transcript.sample_ext() (line 670) — CPU Poseidon2 sponge\n    ├─ do_sumcheck_round_and_revert() (line 698) — GPU kernel + observe_and_update\n    └─ match backend:\n         ├─ FoldEval: for xi_j { do_fused_sumcheck_round() } — GPU + observe_and_update per inner round\n         └─ PrecomputeM: precompute_m → multifold → observe_and_update (with CPU eq_table deps)\n```\n\n### Per-round observe_and_update (fractional.rs:318-346)\n\nEach call does:\n1. **D2H sync**: `d_sum_evals.to_host()` — copies 2 EF (32 bytes) from GPU, BLOCKS until preceding GPU kernel completes\n2. **CPU arithmetic**: `reconstruct_s_evals()` — ~12 EF field operations (inverse, interpolation)\n3. **CPU transcript**: `transcript.observe_ext()` × 3 — absorbs 12 BabyBear elements, triggers 1-2 Poseidon2 permutations (~10μs each)\n4. **CPU transcript**: `transcript.sample_ext()` — squeezes 4 BabyBear elements, triggers 0-1 Poseidon2 permutations\n5. **CPU accumulator update**: `eq_r_acc *= ...`, `prev_s_eval = ...`\n6. **Return**: challenge `r` used by next round's GPU kernel (passed as scalar argument)\n\n### Why it's slow\n\nnsight profiling of the fractional sumcheck kernel timeline shows:\n- Segment 0: 80.5ms wall time, 14.2ms of inter-kernel gaps across ~41 major kernel transitions (mean gap: 0.35ms)\n- Segment 1: 80.3ms wall time, 14.0ms of inter-kernel gaps\n- Each gap is a CPU-GPU roundtrip: GPU idles while CPU does D2H + reconstruct + transcript + next kernel launch\n\n### Existing GPU infrastructure\n\n- **GPU Poseidon2 permutation**: `poseidon2_mix(Fp *cells)` in `crates/cuda-common/include/poseidon2.cuh:184-202`\n- **GPU sponge functions**: `sponge_observe()` and `sponge_sample()` as `__device__` functions in `crates/cuda-backend/cuda/src/sponge.cu:13-42` (must be extracted to shared header)\n- **DeviceSpongeState struct**: defined in both Rust (`sponge.rs:32-39`) and CUDA (`sponge.cu`), `#[repr(C)]` ABI-compatible\n- **Host-device sync**: `DuplexSpongeGpu::sync_h2d()` at `sponge.rs:188` converts Challenger → DeviceSpongeState and uploads\n- **FpExt arithmetic**: Extension field arithmetic already exists in CUDA for GKR kernels (`FracExt` type with multiply, add). Check `fpext.h` or equivalent for `reciprocal()`.\n\n## Changes\n\n### Scope: FoldEval path only (initial prototype)\n\nThe PrecomputeM path (fractional.rs:817+) uses per-round challenges in CPU-side `eval_mle_table` to build eq tables. Moving this to GPU requires additional scope (GPU-side eq table construction). The initial prototype targets the FoldEval path only. When the round strategy selects PrecomputeM, fall back to the existing CPU observe_and_update path.\n\n### Change 1: Extract sponge functions to shared header\n\n**File**: Create `crates/cuda-backend/cuda/include/sponge.cuh` (new header)\n\nExtract from `sponge.cu`:\n- `DeviceSpongeState` struct definition\n- `sponge_observe()` device function\n- `sponge_sample()` device function\n\nUpdate `sponge.cu` to `#include \"sponge.cuh\"` and remove the inlined definitions.\n\n### Change 2: New CUDA kernel `gkr_round_postprocess`\n\n**File**: Add to `crates/cuda-backend/cuda/src/sumcheck.cu` (or new file)\n\n```cuda\n#include \"sponge.cuh\"\n#include \"fpext.h\"  // FpExt arithmetic\n\n__global__ void gkr_round_postprocess(\n    const FpExt *d_sum_evals,      // 2 EF values from compute_round kernel\n    DeviceSpongeState *sponge,     // transcript sponge state (modified in-place)\n    FpExt *d_prev_s_eval,          // scalar accumulator\n    FpExt *d_eq_r_acc,             // scalar accumulator\n    FpExt xi_j,                    // constant per call (passed by value)\n    FpExt *d_challenge_out,        // output: sampled challenge r\n    FpExt *d_s_evals_out           // output: 3 EF for round_polys_eval\n) {\n    // Single thread: all work is sequential, tiny data\n    FpExt eq_r_acc = *d_eq_r_acc;\n    FpExt prev_s_eval = *d_prev_s_eval;\n    \n    // 1. Reconstruct s_evals (matches fractional.rs:1126-1163)\n    FpExt sp[3];\n    sp[1] = d_sum_evals[0] * eq_r_acc;\n    sp[2] = d_sum_evals[1] * eq_r_acc;\n    FpExt eq_xi_0 = FpExt::one() - xi_j;\n    sp[0] = (prev_s_eval - xi_j * sp[1]) * reciprocal(eq_xi_0);\n    \n    // s(X) = eq(xi_j, X) * sp(X)\n    // eq(xi_j, x) = xi_j * x + (1-xi_j) * (1-x)\n    FpExt s_evals[3];\n    for (int i = 0; i < 3; i++) {\n        FpExt x = FpExt::from_u32(i + 1);\n        FpExt sp_x = (i < 2) ? sp[i + 1] : lagrange_interp_012(sp, x);\n        FpExt eq_val = xi_j * x + eq_xi_0 * (FpExt::one() - x);\n        s_evals[i] = eq_val * sp_x;\n    }\n    d_s_evals_out[0] = s_evals[0];\n    d_s_evals_out[1] = s_evals[1];\n    d_s_evals_out[2] = s_evals[2];\n    \n    // 2. Transcript observe: 3 EF = 12 F elements\n    for (int i = 0; i < 3; i++) {\n        for (int c = 0; c < 4; c++) {\n            sponge_observe(*sponge, s_evals[i].coeffs[c]);\n        }\n    }\n    \n    // 3. Transcript sample: 1 EF = 4 F elements\n    FpExt r;\n    for (int c = 0; c < 4; c++) {\n        r.coeffs[c] = sponge_sample(*sponge);\n    }\n    *d_challenge_out = r;\n    \n    // 4. Update accumulators\n    FpExt eq_r_val = xi_j * r + eq_xi_0 * (FpExt::one() - r);\n    *d_eq_r_acc = eq_r_acc * eq_r_val;\n    *d_prev_s_eval = eq_r_val * lagrange_interp_012(sp, r);\n}\n```\n\nAlso implement `lagrange_interp_012(FpExt vals[3], FpExt x)` as a device helper that evaluates the degree-2 Lagrange interpolation through points (0,1,2) at x.\n\n### Change 3: FFI binding for gkr_round_postprocess\n\n**File**: `crates/cuda-backend/src/cuda/logup_zerocheck.rs` (or new cuda module file)\n\n```rust\nextern \"C\" { fn _gkr_round_postprocess(...) -> i32; }\n\npub unsafe fn gkr_round_postprocess(\n    d_sum_evals: &DeviceBuffer<EF>,\n    d_sponge: &DeviceBuffer<DeviceSpongeState>,\n    d_prev_s_eval: &DeviceBuffer<EF>,\n    d_eq_r_acc: &DeviceBuffer<EF>,\n    xi_j: EF,\n    d_challenge_out: *mut EF,  // pointer into pre-allocated challenge array\n    d_s_evals_out: *mut EF,    // pointer into pre-allocated s_evals array\n) -> Result<(), CudaError> { ... }\n```\n\n### Change 4: Modify compute_round kernels to read challenge from device pointer\n\n**Files**: \n- `crates/cuda-backend/cuda/src/sumcheck.cu` — kernel signatures\n- `crates/cuda-backend/src/cuda/logup_zerocheck.rs` — FFI wrappers\n\nFor `frac_compute_round_and_fold` and `frac_compute_round_and_fold_inplace`:\n- Add a `const FpExt *d_r_prev` parameter (device pointer to previous round's challenge)\n- At kernel start: `FpExt r_prev = *d_r_prev;` (L1 cache hit, negligible latency)\n- Remove the scalar `r_prev` parameter\n\nKeep the existing scalar-argument versions as fallback (for PrecomputeM path and first round where challenge comes from CPU).\n\n### Change 5: Modify FoldEval round loop in fractional_sumcheck_gpu\n\n**File**: `crates/cuda-backend/src/logup_zerocheck/fractional.rs`\n\nBefore the outer round loop:\n```rust\n// Allocate device buffers for GPU-side transcript processing\nlet d_sponge = transcript.sync_h2d()?;  // Upload current sponge state\nlet d_prev_s_eval = DeviceBuffer::<EF>::with_capacity(1);\nlet d_eq_r_acc = DeviceBuffer::<EF>::with_capacity(1);\nlet max_inner_rounds = total_rounds;\nlet d_challenges = DeviceBuffer::<EF>::with_capacity(max_inner_rounds * total_rounds);\nlet d_s_evals_array = DeviceBuffer::<EF>::with_capacity(max_inner_rounds * total_rounds * 3);\n```\n\nFor the FoldEval inner loop, replace `observe_and_update()` with:\n```rust\ngkr_round_postprocess(\n    &d_sum_evals,\n    &d_sponge,\n    &d_prev_s_eval,\n    &d_eq_r_acc,\n    xi_j,\n    d_challenges.as_mut_ptr().add(challenge_idx),\n    d_s_evals_array.as_mut_ptr().add(s_eval_idx * 3),\n)?;\nchallenge_idx += 1;\ns_eval_idx += 1;\n```\n\nPass `d_challenges.as_ptr().add(challenge_idx - 1)` as the device pointer for the next compute_round kernel's `r_prev` argument.\n\nFor PrecomputeM rounds: fall back to existing `observe_and_update()` CPU path. Before the PrecomputeM block, D2H the current sponge state (`sync_d2h`) and accumulators. After PrecomputeM, re-upload (`sync_h2d`).\n\n### Change 6: Post-loop CPU transcript replay\n\n**File**: `crates/cuda-backend/src/logup_zerocheck/fractional.rs`\n\nAfter the outer round loop completes:\n```rust\n// D2H: retrieve all round results\nlet all_s_evals = d_s_evals_array.to_host()?;  // total_inner_rounds × 3 EF\nlet all_challenges = d_challenges.to_host()?;   // total_inner_rounds EF\n\n// Replay transcript operations on CPU to synchronize transcript state\nfor i in 0..total_inner_rounds {\n    let s_evals = &all_s_evals[i*3 .. (i+1)*3];\n    for &eval in s_evals {\n        transcript.observe_ext(eval);\n    }\n    let _r = transcript.sample_ext();\n    // Verify: debug_assert_eq!(_r, all_challenges[i]);\n}\n\n// Populate round_polys_eval and r_vec from D2H'd data\nfor i in 0..total_inner_rounds {\n    round_polys_eval.push([all_s_evals[i*3], all_s_evals[i*3+1], all_s_evals[i*3+2]]);\n    r_vec.push(all_challenges[i]);\n}\n```\n\nThis avoids implementing `sync_d2h` entirely. The CPU transcript replay is cheap (~50μs for ~400 sponge operations) and guarantees the CPU transcript state is correct for subsequent phases.\n\nA `debug_assert_eq!` on each replayed challenge vs. the GPU-produced challenge serves as a built-in correctness check.\n\n### Change 7: Implement sync_h2d for DuplexSpongeGpu from fractional_sumcheck_gpu\n\n**File**: `crates/cuda-backend/src/logup_zerocheck/fractional.rs`\n\nThe `fractional_sumcheck_gpu` function is generic over `TS: FiatShamirTranscript<SC>`. To access `DuplexSpongeGpu::sync_h2d()`, we need to either:\n\n**Option A (recommended)**: Add a `d_sponge: &DeviceBuffer<DeviceSpongeState>` parameter to `fractional_sumcheck_gpu`. The caller (which has access to the concrete `DuplexSpongeGpu` type) performs the sync_h2d before calling and passes the device pointer. This avoids changing the generic signature.\n\n**Option B**: Add a trait bound like `TS: FiatShamirTranscript<SC> + HasDeviceSponge`. This is cleaner but changes the API surface.\n\nOption A is minimal: the caller at `prove_zerocheck_and_logup_gpu` (mod.rs:489) already has access to the DuplexSpongeGpu and can sync + pass the device buffer.\n\n## Invariants\n\n1. **Transcript determinism**: The GPU Poseidon2 sponge must produce identical results to the CPU sponge. This is verified by the debug_assert_eq on replayed challenges (Change 6). The sponge functions in `sponge.cu` use the same constants and algorithm as `poseidon2.cuh`, which matches the CPU implementation.\n\n2. **No protocol change**: Same challenges produced in same order. Only execution location changes.\n\n3. **FoldEval-only scope**: PrecomputeM rounds use the existing CPU path (with sync overhead). This ensures the optimization is safe for the initial prototype. The PrecomputeM path accounts for a minority of inner rounds (typically the last few per outer round).\n\n4. **Sponge state consistency**: After the GKR round loop, the CPU transcript is synchronized via replay (Change 6), not via sync_d2h. The replay produces the exact same state because the transcript is deterministic (same inputs → same outputs).\n\n5. **No APC 0 regression**: At APC 0, fewer GKR rounds means less overhead to eliminate. The fixed cost of buffer allocation and post-loop replay is small (~0.1ms). The optimization should be neutral-to-positive.\n\n## Measurement Plan\n\nBuild and run the standard benchmarks:\n```bash\ncd /home/georg/powdr/results/pairing\ncargo build --bin powdr_openvm_riscv -r --features \"metrics,cuda\"\nPROVE_BIN=$(cargo metadata --format-version 1 --no-deps 2>/dev/null | python3 -c 'import sys,json; print(json.load(sys.stdin)[\"target_directory\"])')/release/powdr_openvm_riscv\n$PROVE_BIN prove --artifact apc300.cbor --input 0 --metrics <path>/after_apc300.json --recursion\n$PROVE_BIN prove --artifact apc000.cbor --input 0 --metrics <path>/after_apc000.json --recursion\n```\n\nAnalyze with `python3 /home/georg/spec.py <metrics_path> <name>`.\n\nRun nsight profiling to verify inter-kernel gap reduction:\n```bash\nnsys profile --output <output> --force-overwrite true --trace cuda,nvtx,osrt --sample none --stats true -- $PROVE_BIN prove --artifact apc300.cbor --input 0 --recursion\n```\n\n### Expected results\n- LogUp GKR at APC 300: 526ms → ~500ms (FoldEval rounds only; ~26ms from FoldEval gaps eliminated, PrecomputeM gaps remain)\n- STARK excl trace at APC 300: 1296ms → ~1270ms\n- Fractional sumcheck FoldEval inter-kernel gaps: 14ms → ~3ms per segment (residual from kernel launch overhead + PrecomputeM fallback gaps)\n- No regression at APC 0\n- All debug_assert_eq on replayed challenges pass\n\n### Verification\n- Proof verification must pass at APC 0, 100, and 300\n- Debug assertions on GPU-vs-CPU challenge comparison must hold\n\n## Rollback Criteria\n\nRevert if:\n- LogUp GKR improvement at APC 300 is less than 10ms\n- Any regression at APC 0 exceeds 10ms on STARK excl trace\n- Proof verification fails at any APC configuration\n- GPU-produced challenges differ from CPU reference (debug_assert fires)\n"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2977,"cache_read_input_tokens":236412,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":2977},"output_tokens":65,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"e33616ae-361c-4ad4-bcb1-e94f2f710e44","uuid":"4c88ca7b-1bfe-4642-8dfe-6de37dac3237"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01VDhLMbzgkzDXMq8HEhEPAN","type":"tool_result","content":"The file /home/georg/stark-backend/autoopt-results/2026-04-14-1800-gpu-gkr-transcript-processing/plan.md has been updated successfully."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"e33616ae-361c-4ad4-bcb1-e94f2f710e44","uuid":"d817560f-6a44-4d21-9dfd-154187275e13","timestamp":"2026-04-14T16:35:35.326Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"update","filePath":"/home/georg/stark-backend/autoopt-results/2026-04-14-1800-gpu-gkr-transcript-processing/plan.md","content":"# Plan: GPU-Side GKR Transcript Processing\n\n## Goal\n\nEliminate per-round CPU-GPU roundtrips in the GKR fractional sumcheck by moving the `observe_and_update` function (D2H copy + arithmetic + Poseidon2 transcript observe/sample) to a GPU kernel. This targets the 14ms per-segment inter-kernel gap measured by nsight in the fractional sumcheck round loop (28ms total across 2 segments at APC 300).\n\n## Current Code Path\n\n### Call chain\n\n```\nfractional_sumcheck_gpu() @ fractional.rs:491\n└─ for round in 1..total_rounds (line 655)\n    ├─ eq_buffer = SqrtEqLayers::from_xi() (line 663) — CPU\n    ├─ lambda = transcript.sample_ext() (line 670) — CPU Poseidon2 sponge\n    ├─ do_sumcheck_round_and_revert() (line 698) — GPU kernel + observe_and_update\n    └─ match backend:\n         ├─ FoldEval: for xi_j { do_fused_sumcheck_round() } — GPU + observe_and_update per inner round\n         └─ PrecomputeM: precompute_m → multifold → observe_and_update (with CPU eq_table deps)\n```\n\n### Per-round observe_and_update (fractional.rs:318-346)\n\nEach call does:\n1. **D2H sync**: `d_sum_evals.to_host()` — copies 2 EF (32 bytes) from GPU, BLOCKS until preceding GPU kernel completes\n2. **CPU arithmetic**: `reconstruct_s_evals()` — ~12 EF field operations (inverse, interpolation)\n3. **CPU transcript**: `transcript.observe_ext()` × 3 — absorbs 12 BabyBear elements, triggers 1-2 Poseidon2 permutations (~10μs each)\n4. **CPU transcript**: `transcript.sample_ext()` — squeezes 4 BabyBear elements, triggers 0-1 Poseidon2 permutations\n5. **CPU accumulator update**: `eq_r_acc *= ...`, `prev_s_eval = ...`\n6. **Return**: challenge `r` used by next round's GPU kernel (passed as scalar argument)\n\n### Why it's slow\n\nnsight profiling of the fractional sumcheck kernel timeline shows:\n- Segment 0: 80.5ms wall time, 14.2ms of inter-kernel gaps across ~41 major kernel transitions (mean gap: 0.35ms)\n- Segment 1: 80.3ms wall time, 14.0ms of inter-kernel gaps\n- Each gap is a CPU-GPU roundtrip: GPU idles while CPU does D2H + reconstruct + transcript + next kernel launch\n\n### Existing GPU infrastructure\n\n- **GPU Poseidon2 permutation**: `poseidon2_mix(Fp *cells)` in `crates/cuda-common/include/poseidon2.cuh:184-202`\n- **GPU sponge functions**: `sponge_observe()` and `sponge_sample()` as `__device__` functions in `crates/cuda-backend/cuda/src/sponge.cu:13-42` (must be extracted to shared header)\n- **DeviceSpongeState struct**: defined in both Rust (`sponge.rs:32-39`) and CUDA (`sponge.cu`), `#[repr(C)]` ABI-compatible\n- **Host-device sync**: `DuplexSpongeGpu::sync_h2d()` at `sponge.rs:188` converts Challenger → DeviceSpongeState and uploads\n- **FpExt arithmetic**: Extension field arithmetic already exists in CUDA for GKR kernels (`FracExt` type with multiply, add). Check `fpext.h` or equivalent for `reciprocal()`.\n\n## Changes\n\n### Scope: FoldEval path only (initial prototype)\n\nThe PrecomputeM path (fractional.rs:817+) uses per-round challenges in CPU-side `eval_mle_table` to build eq tables. Moving this to GPU requires additional scope (GPU-side eq table construction). The initial prototype targets the FoldEval path only. When the round strategy selects PrecomputeM, fall back to the existing CPU observe_and_update path.\n\n### Change 1: Extract sponge functions to shared header\n\n**File**: Create `crates/cuda-backend/cuda/include/sponge.cuh` (new header)\n\nExtract from `sponge.cu`:\n- `DeviceSpongeState` struct definition\n- `sponge_observe()` device function\n- `sponge_sample()` device function\n\nUpdate `sponge.cu` to `#include \"sponge.cuh\"` and remove the inlined definitions.\n\n### Change 2: New CUDA kernel `gkr_round_postprocess`\n\n**File**: Add to `crates/cuda-backend/cuda/src/sumcheck.cu` (or new file)\n\n```cuda\n#include \"sponge.cuh\"\n#include \"fpext.h\"  // FpExt arithmetic\n\n__global__ void gkr_round_postprocess(\n    const FpExt *d_sum_evals,      // 2 EF values from compute_round kernel\n    DeviceSpongeState *sponge,     // transcript sponge state (modified in-place)\n    FpExt *d_prev_s_eval,          // scalar accumulator\n    FpExt *d_eq_r_acc,             // scalar accumulator\n    FpExt xi_j,                    // constant per call (passed by value)\n    FpExt *d_challenge_out,        // output: sampled challenge r\n    FpExt *d_s_evals_out           // output: 3 EF for round_polys_eval\n) {\n    // Single thread: all work is sequential, tiny data\n    FpExt eq_r_acc = *d_eq_r_acc;\n    FpExt prev_s_eval = *d_prev_s_eval;\n    \n    // 1. Reconstruct s_evals (matches fractional.rs:1126-1163)\n    FpExt sp[3];\n    sp[1] = d_sum_evals[0] * eq_r_acc;\n    sp[2] = d_sum_evals[1] * eq_r_acc;\n    FpExt eq_xi_0 = FpExt::one() - xi_j;\n    sp[0] = (prev_s_eval - xi_j * sp[1]) * reciprocal(eq_xi_0);\n    \n    // s(X) = eq(xi_j, X) * sp(X)\n    // eq(xi_j, x) = xi_j * x + (1-xi_j) * (1-x)\n    FpExt s_evals[3];\n    for (int i = 0; i < 3; i++) {\n        FpExt x = FpExt::from_u32(i + 1);\n        FpExt sp_x = (i < 2) ? sp[i + 1] : lagrange_interp_012(sp, x);\n        FpExt eq_val = xi_j * x + eq_xi_0 * (FpExt::one() - x);\n        s_evals[i] = eq_val * sp_x;\n    }\n    d_s_evals_out[0] = s_evals[0];\n    d_s_evals_out[1] = s_evals[1];\n    d_s_evals_out[2] = s_evals[2];\n    \n    // 2. Transcript observe: 3 EF = 12 F elements\n    for (int i = 0; i < 3; i++) {\n        for (int c = 0; c < 4; c++) {\n            sponge_observe(*sponge, s_evals[i].coeffs[c]);\n        }\n    }\n    \n    // 3. Transcript sample: 1 EF = 4 F elements\n    FpExt r;\n    for (int c = 0; c < 4; c++) {\n        r.coeffs[c] = sponge_sample(*sponge);\n    }\n    *d_challenge_out = r;\n    \n    // 4. Update accumulators\n    FpExt eq_r_val = xi_j * r + eq_xi_0 * (FpExt::one() - r);\n    *d_eq_r_acc = eq_r_acc * eq_r_val;\n    *d_prev_s_eval = eq_r_val * lagrange_interp_012(sp, r);\n}\n```\n\nAlso implement `lagrange_interp_012(FpExt vals[3], FpExt x)` as a device helper that evaluates the degree-2 Lagrange interpolation through points (0,1,2) at x.\n\n### Change 3: FFI binding for gkr_round_postprocess\n\n**File**: `crates/cuda-backend/src/cuda/logup_zerocheck.rs` (or new cuda module file)\n\n```rust\nextern \"C\" { fn _gkr_round_postprocess(...) -> i32; }\n\npub unsafe fn gkr_round_postprocess(\n    d_sum_evals: &DeviceBuffer<EF>,\n    d_sponge: &DeviceBuffer<DeviceSpongeState>,\n    d_prev_s_eval: &DeviceBuffer<EF>,\n    d_eq_r_acc: &DeviceBuffer<EF>,\n    xi_j: EF,\n    d_challenge_out: *mut EF,  // pointer into pre-allocated challenge array\n    d_s_evals_out: *mut EF,    // pointer into pre-allocated s_evals array\n) -> Result<(), CudaError> { ... }\n```\n\n### Change 4: Modify compute_round kernels to read challenge from device pointer\n\n**Files**: \n- `crates/cuda-backend/cuda/src/sumcheck.cu` — kernel signatures\n- `crates/cuda-backend/src/cuda/logup_zerocheck.rs` — FFI wrappers\n\nFor `frac_compute_round_and_fold` and `frac_compute_round_and_fold_inplace`:\n- Add a `const FpExt *d_r_prev` parameter (device pointer to previous round's challenge)\n- At kernel start: `FpExt r_prev = *d_r_prev;` (L1 cache hit, negligible latency)\n- Remove the scalar `r_prev` parameter\n\nKeep the existing scalar-argument versions as fallback (for PrecomputeM path and first round where challenge comes from CPU).\n\n### Change 5: Modify FoldEval round loop in fractional_sumcheck_gpu\n\n**File**: `crates/cuda-backend/src/logup_zerocheck/fractional.rs`\n\nBefore the outer round loop:\n```rust\n// Allocate device buffers for GPU-side transcript processing\nlet d_sponge = transcript.sync_h2d()?;  // Upload current sponge state\nlet d_prev_s_eval = DeviceBuffer::<EF>::with_capacity(1);\nlet d_eq_r_acc = DeviceBuffer::<EF>::with_capacity(1);\nlet max_inner_rounds = total_rounds;\nlet d_challenges = DeviceBuffer::<EF>::with_capacity(max_inner_rounds * total_rounds);\nlet d_s_evals_array = DeviceBuffer::<EF>::with_capacity(max_inner_rounds * total_rounds * 3);\n```\n\nFor the FoldEval inner loop, replace `observe_and_update()` with:\n```rust\ngkr_round_postprocess(\n    &d_sum_evals,\n    &d_sponge,\n    &d_prev_s_eval,\n    &d_eq_r_acc,\n    xi_j,\n    d_challenges.as_mut_ptr().add(challenge_idx),\n    d_s_evals_array.as_mut_ptr().add(s_eval_idx * 3),\n)?;\nchallenge_idx += 1;\ns_eval_idx += 1;\n```\n\nPass `d_challenges.as_ptr().add(challenge_idx - 1)` as the device pointer for the next compute_round kernel's `r_prev` argument.\n\nFor PrecomputeM rounds: fall back to existing `observe_and_update()` CPU path. Before the PrecomputeM block, D2H the current sponge state (`sync_d2h`) and accumulators. After PrecomputeM, re-upload (`sync_h2d`).\n\n### Change 6: Post-loop CPU transcript replay\n\n**File**: `crates/cuda-backend/src/logup_zerocheck/fractional.rs`\n\nAfter the outer round loop completes:\n```rust\n// D2H: retrieve all round results\nlet all_s_evals = d_s_evals_array.to_host()?;  // total_inner_rounds × 3 EF\nlet all_challenges = d_challenges.to_host()?;   // total_inner_rounds EF\n\n// Replay transcript operations on CPU to synchronize transcript state\nfor i in 0..total_inner_rounds {\n    let s_evals = &all_s_evals[i*3 .. (i+1)*3];\n    for &eval in s_evals {\n        transcript.observe_ext(eval);\n    }\n    let _r = transcript.sample_ext();\n    // Verify: debug_assert_eq!(_r, all_challenges[i]);\n}\n\n// Populate round_polys_eval and r_vec from D2H'd data\nfor i in 0..total_inner_rounds {\n    round_polys_eval.push([all_s_evals[i*3], all_s_evals[i*3+1], all_s_evals[i*3+2]]);\n    r_vec.push(all_challenges[i]);\n}\n```\n\nThis avoids implementing `sync_d2h` entirely. The CPU transcript replay is cheap (~50μs for ~400 sponge operations) and guarantees the CPU transcript state is correct for subsequent phases.\n\nA `debug_assert_eq!` on each replayed challenge vs. the GPU-produced challenge serves as a built-in correctness check.\n\n### Change 7: Implement sync_h2d for DuplexSpongeGpu from fractional_sumcheck_gpu\n\n**File**: `crates/cuda-backend/src/logup_zerocheck/fractional.rs`\n\nThe `fractional_sumcheck_gpu` function is generic over `TS: FiatShamirTranscript<SC>`. To access `DuplexSpongeGpu::sync_h2d()`, we need to either:\n\n**Option A (recommended)**: Add a `d_sponge: &DeviceBuffer<DeviceSpongeState>` parameter to `fractional_sumcheck_gpu`. The caller (which has access to the concrete `DuplexSpongeGpu` type) performs the sync_h2d before calling and passes the device pointer. This avoids changing the generic signature.\n\n**Option B**: Add a trait bound like `TS: FiatShamirTranscript<SC> + HasDeviceSponge`. This is cleaner but changes the API surface.\n\nOption A is minimal: the caller at `prove_zerocheck_and_logup_gpu` (mod.rs:489) already has access to the DuplexSpongeGpu and can sync + pass the device buffer.\n\n## Invariants\n\n1. **Transcript determinism**: The GPU Poseidon2 sponge must produce identical results to the CPU sponge. This is verified by the debug_assert_eq on replayed challenges (Change 6). The sponge functions in `sponge.cu` use the same constants and algorithm as `poseidon2.cuh`, which matches the CPU implementation.\n\n2. **No protocol change**: Same challenges produced in same order. Only execution location changes.\n\n3. **FoldEval-only scope**: PrecomputeM rounds use the existing CPU path (with sync overhead). This ensures the optimization is safe for the initial prototype. The PrecomputeM path accounts for a minority of inner rounds (typically the last few per outer round).\n\n4. **Sponge state consistency**: After the GKR round loop, the CPU transcript is synchronized via replay (Change 6), not via sync_d2h. The replay produces the exact same state because the transcript is deterministic (same inputs → same outputs).\n\n5. **No APC 0 regression**: At APC 0, fewer GKR rounds means less overhead to eliminate. The fixed cost of buffer allocation and post-loop replay is small (~0.1ms). The optimization should be neutral-to-positive.\n\n## Measurement Plan\n\nBuild and run the standard benchmarks:\n```bash\ncd /home/georg/powdr/results/pairing\ncargo build --bin powdr_openvm_riscv -r --features \"metrics,cuda\"\nPROVE_BIN=$(cargo metadata --format-version 1 --no-deps 2>/dev/null | python3 -c 'import sys,json; print(json.load(sys.stdin)[\"target_directory\"])')/release/powdr_openvm_riscv\n$PROVE_BIN prove --artifact apc300.cbor --input 0 --metrics <path>/after_apc300.json --recursion\n$PROVE_BIN prove --artifact apc000.cbor --input 0 --metrics <path>/after_apc000.json --recursion\n```\n\nAnalyze with `python3 /home/georg/spec.py <metrics_path> <name>`.\n\nRun nsight profiling to verify inter-kernel gap reduction:\n```bash\nnsys profile --output <output> --force-overwrite true --trace cuda,nvtx,osrt --sample none --stats true -- $PROVE_BIN prove --artifact apc300.cbor --input 0 --recursion\n```\n\n### Expected results\n- LogUp GKR at APC 300: 526ms → ~500ms (FoldEval rounds only; ~26ms from FoldEval gaps eliminated, PrecomputeM gaps remain)\n- STARK excl trace at APC 300: 1296ms → ~1270ms\n- Fractional sumcheck FoldEval inter-kernel gaps: 14ms → ~3ms per segment (residual from kernel launch overhead + PrecomputeM fallback gaps)\n- No regression at APC 0\n- All debug_assert_eq on replayed challenges pass\n\n### Verification\n- Proof verification must pass at APC 0, 100, and 300\n- Debug assertions on GPU-vs-CPU challenge comparison must hold\n\n## Rollback Criteria\n\nRevert if:\n- LogUp GKR improvement at APC 300 is less than 10ms\n- Any regression at APC 0 exceeds 10ms on STARK excl trace\n- Proof verification fails at any APC configuration\n- GPU-produced challenges differ from CPU reference (debug_assert fires)\n","structuredPatch":[{"oldStart":16,"oldLines":7,"newStart":16,"newLines":7,"lines":["     ├─ do_sumcheck_round_and_revert() (line 698) — GPU kernel + observe_and_update","     └─ match backend:","          ├─ FoldEval: for xi_j { do_fused_sumcheck_round() } — GPU + observe_and_update per inner round","-         └─ PrecomputeM: precompute_m → multifold → observe_and_update (fewer calls)","+         └─ PrecomputeM: precompute_m → multifold → observe_and_update (with CPU eq_table deps)"," ```"," "," ### Per-round observe_and_update (fractional.rs:318-346)"]},{"oldStart":24,"oldLines":7,"newStart":24,"newLines":7,"lines":[" Each call does:"," 1. **D2H sync**: `d_sum_evals.to_host()` — copies 2 EF (32 bytes) from GPU, BLOCKS until preceding GPU kernel completes"," 2. **CPU arithmetic**: `reconstruct_s_evals()` — ~12 EF field operations (inverse, interpolation)","-3. **CPU transcript**: `transcript.observe_ext()` × 3 — absorbs 12 BabyBear elements, triggers 1-2 Poseidon2 permutations","+3. **CPU transcript**: `transcript.observe_ext()` × 3 — absorbs 12 BabyBear elements, triggers 1-2 Poseidon2 permutations (~10μs each)"," 4. **CPU transcript**: `transcript.sample_ext()` — squeezes 4 BabyBear elements, triggers 0-1 Poseidon2 permutations"," 5. **CPU accumulator update**: `eq_r_acc *= ...`, `prev_s_eval = ...`"," 6. **Return**: challenge `r` used by next round's GPU kernel (passed as scalar argument)"]},{"oldStart":34,"oldLines":71,"newStart":34,"newLines":82,"lines":[" nsight profiling of the fractional sumcheck kernel timeline shows:"," - Segment 0: 80.5ms wall time, 14.2ms of inter-kernel gaps across ~41 major kernel transitions (mean gap: 0.35ms)"," - Segment 1: 80.3ms wall time, 14.0ms of inter-kernel gaps","+- Each gap is a CPU-GPU roundtrip: GPU idles while CPU does D2H + reconstruct + transcript + next kernel launch"," ","-Each gap is a CPU-GPU roundtrip: the GPU idles while the CPU does D2H + reconstruct + transcript + launch next kernel. The GPU compute kernels themselves (compute_round_and_revert, compute_round_and_fold_inplace, multifold, precompute_m) total only ~119ms GPU time per segment.","-"," ### Existing GPU infrastructure"," ","-The codebase already has:"," - **GPU Poseidon2 permutation**: `poseidon2_mix(Fp *cells)` in `crates/cuda-common/include/poseidon2.cuh:184-202`","-- **GPU sponge state**: `DeviceSpongeState { Fp state[16]; uint32_t absorb_idx; uint32_t sample_idx; }` in `poseidon2.cuh`","-- **GPU sponge operations**: `sponge_observe(state, val)` and `sponge_sample(state)` device functions in `poseidon2.cuh:24-42`","-- **Host-device sponge sync**: `DuplexSpongeGpu` in `crates/cuda-backend/src/sponge.rs` already synchronizes sponge state between host and device (used by `grind_gpu`)","+- **GPU sponge functions**: `sponge_observe()` and `sponge_sample()` as `__device__` functions in `crates/cuda-backend/cuda/src/sponge.cu:13-42` (must be extracted to shared header)","+- **DeviceSpongeState struct**: defined in both Rust (`sponge.rs:32-39`) and CUDA (`sponge.cu`), `#[repr(C)]` ABI-compatible","+- **Host-device sync**: `DuplexSpongeGpu::sync_h2d()` at `sponge.rs:188` converts Challenger → DeviceSpongeState and uploads","+- **FpExt arithmetic**: Extension field arithmetic already exists in CUDA for GKR kernels (`FracExt` type with multiply, add). Check `fpext.h` or equivalent for `reciprocal()`."," "," ## Changes"," ","-### Change 1: New CUDA kernel `gkr_round_postprocess`","+### Scope: FoldEval path only (initial prototype)"," ","-**File**: `crates/cuda-backend/cuda/src/fractional_sumcheck.cu` (new file, or append to existing sumcheck.cu)","+The PrecomputeM path (fractional.rs:817+) uses per-round challenges in CPU-side `eval_mle_table` to build eq tables. Moving this to GPU requires additional scope (GPU-side eq table construction). The initial prototype targets the FoldEval path only. When the round strategy selects PrecomputeM, fall back to the existing CPU observe_and_update path."," ","-Create a single-thread GPU kernel that replaces `observe_and_update`:","+### Change 1: Extract sponge functions to shared header"," ","+**File**: Create `crates/cuda-backend/cuda/include/sponge.cuh` (new header)","+","+Extract from `sponge.cu`:","+- `DeviceSpongeState` struct definition","+- `sponge_observe()` device function","+- `sponge_sample()` device function","+","+Update `sponge.cu` to `#include \"sponge.cuh\"` and remove the inlined definitions.","+","+### Change 2: New CUDA kernel `gkr_round_postprocess`","+","+**File**: Add to `crates/cuda-backend/cuda/src/sumcheck.cu` (or new file)","+"," ```cuda","+#include \"sponge.cuh\"","+#include \"fpext.h\"  // FpExt arithmetic","+"," __global__ void gkr_round_postprocess(","     const FpExt *d_sum_evals,      // 2 EF values from compute_round kernel","     DeviceSpongeState *sponge,     // transcript sponge state (modified in-place)","-    FpExt *d_prev_s_eval,          // scalar, modified in-place","-    FpExt *d_eq_r_acc,             // scalar, modified in-place","-    FpExt xi_j,                    // constant per call","+    FpExt *d_prev_s_eval,          // scalar accumulator","+    FpExt *d_eq_r_acc,             // scalar accumulator","+    FpExt xi_j,                    // constant per call (passed by value)","     FpExt *d_challenge_out,        // output: sampled challenge r","-    FpExt *d_s_evals_out,          // output: s_evals[0..3] for round_polys_eval","-    FpExt *d_sp_evals_out          // output: sp_evals for debugging/verification (optional)","+    FpExt *d_s_evals_out           // output: 3 EF for round_polys_eval"," ) {","-    // 1. Reconstruct s_evals (same logic as fractional.rs:1126-1163)","+    // Single thread: all work is sequential, tiny data","     FpExt eq_r_acc = *d_eq_r_acc;","     FpExt prev_s_eval = *d_prev_s_eval;","     ","-    FpExt sp_evals[3];","-    sp_evals[1] = d_sum_evals[0] * eq_r_acc;","-    sp_evals[2] = d_sum_evals[1] * eq_r_acc;","+    // 1. Reconstruct s_evals (matches fractional.rs:1126-1163)","+    FpExt sp[3];","+    sp[1] = d_sum_evals[0] * eq_r_acc;","+    sp[2] = d_sum_evals[1] * eq_r_acc;","     FpExt eq_xi_0 = FpExt::one() - xi_j;","-    FpExt eq_xi_1 = xi_j;","-    sp_evals[0] = (prev_s_eval - eq_xi_1 * sp_evals[1]) * inverse(eq_xi_0);","+    sp[0] = (prev_s_eval - xi_j * sp[1]) * reciprocal(eq_xi_0);","     ","-    // Interpolate at X=3 (quadratic through 0,1,2)","-    // sp(3) = sp(0) - 3*sp(1) + 3*sp(2) (Newton form for quadratic)","-    // Actually: Lagrange at points 0,1,2 evaluated at 3","-    ","-    // Compute s_evals[i] = eq(xi_j, i+1) * sp(i+1)","+    // s(X) = eq(xi_j, X) * sp(X)","+    // eq(xi_j, x) = xi_j * x + (1-xi_j) * (1-x)","     FpExt s_evals[3];","     for (int i = 0; i < 3; i++) {","         FpExt x = FpExt::from_u32(i + 1);","-        FpExt sp_x = (i < 2) ? sp_evals[i + 1] : interpolate_quadratic_012(sp_evals, x);","-        s_evals[i] = eval_eq_mle_1(xi_j, x) * sp_x;","+        FpExt sp_x = (i < 2) ? sp[i + 1] : lagrange_interp_012(sp, x);","+        FpExt eq_val = xi_j * x + eq_xi_0 * (FpExt::one() - x);","+        s_evals[i] = eq_val * sp_x;","     }","     d_s_evals_out[0] = s_evals[0];","     d_s_evals_out[1] = s_evals[1];","     d_s_evals_out[2] = s_evals[2];","     ","-    // 2. Transcript observe: 3 EF = 12 F","+    // 2. Transcript observe: 3 EF = 12 F elements","     for (int i = 0; i < 3; i++) {","-        // observe_ext: absorb 4 base field coefficients","         for (int c = 0; c < 4; c++) {","             sponge_observe(*sponge, s_evals[i].coeffs[c]);","         }","     }","     ","-    // 3. Transcript sample: 1 EF = 4 F","+    // 3. Transcript sample: 1 EF = 4 F elements","     FpExt r;","     for (int c = 0; c < 4; c++) {","         r.coeffs[c] = sponge_sample(*sponge);"]},{"oldStart":106,"oldLines":136,"newStart":117,"newLines":162,"lines":["     *d_challenge_out = r;","     ","     // 4. Update accumulators","-    FpExt eq_r = eval_eq_mle_1(xi_j, r);","-    *d_eq_r_acc = eq_r_acc * eq_r;","-    *d_prev_s_eval = eq_r * interpolate_quadratic_012(sp_evals, r);","+    FpExt eq_r_val = xi_j * r + eq_xi_0 * (FpExt::one() - r);","+    *d_eq_r_acc = eq_r_acc * eq_r_val;","+    *d_prev_s_eval = eq_r_val * lagrange_interp_012(sp, r);"," }"," ```"," ","-This kernel runs on 1 thread (<<<1,1>>>). It needs:","-- `FpExt` (BinomialExtensionField<BabyBear, 4>) arithmetic in CUDA: add, sub, mul, inverse","-- `eval_eq_mle_1(xi, x)` = `xi * x + (1-xi) * (1-x)` — trivial","-- `interpolate_quadratic_012(vals, x)` — Lagrange interpolation of degree-2 poly through points (0,1,2) evaluated at x","-- Existing `sponge_observe` and `sponge_sample` from poseidon2.cuh","+Also implement `lagrange_interp_012(FpExt vals[3], FpExt x)` as a device helper that evaluates the degree-2 Lagrange interpolation through points (0,1,2) at x."," ","-### Change 2: EF arithmetic in CUDA","+### Change 3: FFI binding for gkr_round_postprocess"," ","-**File**: `crates/cuda-common/include/ext_field.cuh` (new or extend existing)","+**File**: `crates/cuda-backend/src/cuda/logup_zerocheck.rs` (or new cuda module file)"," ","-If EF (BinomialExtensionField<BabyBear,4>) arithmetic doesn't already exist in CUDA device code, implement:","-- `FpExt` struct: 4 `Fp` coefficients","-- Add, sub, mul (using the binomial extension multiplication formula)","-- Inverse (using extended Euclidean or Fermat's little theorem)","-- `from_u32`, `one()`, `zero()`","+```rust","+extern \"C\" { fn _gkr_round_postprocess(...) -> i32; }"," ","-Check first: the GKR input evaluation kernel already uses `FracExt` which contains `FpExt` members. The extension field arithmetic likely exists in the codebase.","+pub unsafe fn gkr_round_postprocess(","+    d_sum_evals: &DeviceBuffer<EF>,","+    d_sponge: &DeviceBuffer<DeviceSpongeState>,","+    d_prev_s_eval: &DeviceBuffer<EF>,","+    d_eq_r_acc: &DeviceBuffer<EF>,","+    xi_j: EF,","+    d_challenge_out: *mut EF,  // pointer into pre-allocated challenge array","+    d_s_evals_out: *mut EF,    // pointer into pre-allocated s_evals array","+) -> Result<(), CudaError> { ... }","+```"," ","-### Change 3: FFI wrapper for gkr_round_postprocess","+### Change 4: Modify compute_round kernels to read challenge from device pointer"," ","-**File**: `crates/cuda-backend/src/cuda/fractional.rs` (new, or extend existing cuda module)","+**Files**: ","+- `crates/cuda-backend/cuda/src/sumcheck.cu` — kernel signatures","+- `crates/cuda-backend/src/cuda/logup_zerocheck.rs` — FFI wrappers"," ","-Add Rust FFI binding:","-```rust","-extern \"C\" {","-    fn _gkr_round_postprocess(","-        d_sum_evals: *const EF,","-        sponge: *mut DeviceSpongeState,","-        d_prev_s_eval: *mut EF,","-        d_eq_r_acc: *mut EF,","-        xi_j: EF,  // passed by value (16 bytes)","-        d_challenge_out: *mut EF,","-        d_s_evals_out: *mut EF,","-        d_sp_evals_out: *mut EF,","-    ) -> i32;","-}","-```","+For `frac_compute_round_and_fold` and `frac_compute_round_and_fold_inplace`:","+- Add a `const FpExt *d_r_prev` parameter (device pointer to previous round's challenge)","+- At kernel start: `FpExt r_prev = *d_r_prev;` (L1 cache hit, negligible latency)","+- Remove the scalar `r_prev` parameter"," ","-### Change 4: Modify fractional sumcheck round loop","+Keep the existing scalar-argument versions as fallback (for PrecomputeM path and first round where challenge comes from CPU)."," ","+### Change 5: Modify FoldEval round loop in fractional_sumcheck_gpu","+"," **File**: `crates/cuda-backend/src/logup_zerocheck/fractional.rs`"," ","-Before the GKR round loop (line 655):","-1. **Upload sponge state**: Sync the current CPU transcript state to a `DeviceBuffer<DeviceSpongeState>` (the existing `DuplexSpongeGpu::sync_h2d()` pattern)","-2. **Allocate device buffers**: `d_prev_s_eval`, `d_eq_r_acc`, `d_challenges` (array of total_rounds EF), `d_round_polys_eval` (array of total_rounds × 3 EF), `d_sp_evals_scratch`","+Before the outer round loop:","+```rust","+// Allocate device buffers for GPU-side transcript processing","+let d_sponge = transcript.sync_h2d()?;  // Upload current sponge state","+let d_prev_s_eval = DeviceBuffer::<EF>::with_capacity(1);","+let d_eq_r_acc = DeviceBuffer::<EF>::with_capacity(1);","+let max_inner_rounds = total_rounds;","+let d_challenges = DeviceBuffer::<EF>::with_capacity(max_inner_rounds * total_rounds);","+let d_s_evals_array = DeviceBuffer::<EF>::with_capacity(max_inner_rounds * total_rounds * 3);","+```"," ","-Replace `observe_and_update()` calls (lines 382, and within do_fused_sumcheck_round and PrecomputeM paths) with:","+For the FoldEval inner loop, replace `observe_and_update()` with:"," ```rust","-unsafe { gkr_round_postprocess(","-    d_sum_evals.as_ptr(),","-    d_sponge.as_mut_ptr(),","-    d_prev_s_eval.as_mut_ptr(),","-    d_eq_r_acc.as_mut_ptr(),","+gkr_round_postprocess(","+    &d_sum_evals,","+    &d_sponge,","+    &d_prev_s_eval,","+    &d_eq_r_acc,","     xi_j,","     d_challenges.as_mut_ptr().add(challenge_idx),","-    d_round_polys_ptr.add(round_polys_idx * 3),","-    d_sp_evals_scratch.as_mut_ptr(),","-)?; }","+    d_s_evals_array.as_mut_ptr().add(s_eval_idx * 3),","+)?;","+challenge_idx += 1;","+s_eval_idx += 1;"," ```"," ","-Modify the next round's compute kernel to read the challenge from `d_challenges[challenge_idx]` instead of receiving it as a scalar argument. The challenge is already on device — pass a device pointer.","+Pass `d_challenges.as_ptr().add(challenge_idx - 1)` as the device pointer for the next compute_round kernel's `r_prev` argument."," ","-### Change 5: Post-loop D2H and CPU state synchronization","+For PrecomputeM rounds: fall back to existing `observe_and_update()` CPU path. Before the PrecomputeM block, D2H the current sponge state (`sync_d2h`) and accumulators. After PrecomputeM, re-upload (`sync_h2d`)."," ","+### Change 6: Post-loop CPU transcript replay","+"," **File**: `crates/cuda-backend/src/logup_zerocheck/fractional.rs`"," ","-After the GKR round loop completes:","-1. **D2H**: Copy `d_round_polys_eval`, `d_challenges` (r_vec), `d_eq_r_acc`, `d_prev_s_eval`, `d_sponge` back to host","-2. **Update CPU transcript**: Restore the CPU transcript's sponge state from the downloaded GPU sponge state (reverse of sync_h2d)","-3. **Populate CPU data structures**: Fill `round_polys_eval` and `r_vec` from the D2H'd arrays","-4. **Continue**: The rest of the proof uses the CPU transcript as before","+After the outer round loop completes:","+```rust","+// D2H: retrieve all round results","+let all_s_evals = d_s_evals_array.to_host()?;  // total_inner_rounds × 3 EF","+let all_challenges = d_challenges.to_host()?;   // total_inner_rounds EF"," ","-### Change 6: Pass challenge by device pointer to compute_round kernels","+// Replay transcript operations on CPU to synchronize transcript state","+for i in 0..total_inner_rounds {","+    let s_evals = &all_s_evals[i*3 .. (i+1)*3];","+    for &eval in s_evals {","+        transcript.observe_ext(eval);","+    }","+    let _r = transcript.sample_ext();","+    // Verify: debug_assert_eq!(_r, all_challenges[i]);","+}"," ","-**Files**: CUDA kernel wrappers in `crates/cuda-backend/src/cuda/logup_zerocheck.rs`, CUDA kernels in `crates/cuda-backend/cuda/src/sumcheck.cu`","-","-Modify `frac_compute_round_and_fold`, `frac_compute_round_and_revert`, etc. to accept the previous round's challenge `r` via device pointer instead of scalar value. Add a `__device__` read at kernel start:","-```cuda","-FpExt r = *d_r_ptr;  // read from device memory (16 bytes, L1 cached)","+// Populate round_polys_eval and r_vec from D2H'd data","+for i in 0..total_inner_rounds {","+    round_polys_eval.push([all_s_evals[i*3], all_s_evals[i*3+1], all_s_evals[i*3+2]]);","+    r_vec.push(all_challenges[i]);","+}"," ```"," ","-This is a minimal change to the existing kernels. The pointer argument replaces the scalar argument. Read latency is negligible (L1 cache hit).","+This avoids implementing `sync_d2h` entirely. The CPU transcript replay is cheap (~50μs for ~400 sponge operations) and guarantees the CPU transcript state is correct for subsequent phases."," ","+A `debug_assert_eq!` on each replayed challenge vs. the GPU-produced challenge serves as a built-in correctness check.","+","+### Change 7: Implement sync_h2d for DuplexSpongeGpu from fractional_sumcheck_gpu","+","+**File**: `crates/cuda-backend/src/logup_zerocheck/fractional.rs`","+","+The `fractional_sumcheck_gpu` function is generic over `TS: FiatShamirTranscript<SC>`. To access `DuplexSpongeGpu::sync_h2d()`, we need to either:","+","+**Option A (recommended)**: Add a `d_sponge: &DeviceBuffer<DeviceSpongeState>` parameter to `fractional_sumcheck_gpu`. The caller (which has access to the concrete `DuplexSpongeGpu` type) performs the sync_h2d before calling and passes the device pointer. This avoids changing the generic signature.","+","+**Option B**: Add a trait bound like `TS: FiatShamirTranscript<SC> + HasDeviceSponge`. This is cleaner but changes the API surface.","+","+Option A is minimal: the caller at `prove_zerocheck_and_logup_gpu` (mod.rs:489) already has access to the DuplexSpongeGpu and can sync + pass the device buffer.","+"," ## Invariants"," ","-1. **Transcript determinism**: The GPU sponge must produce identical observe/sample sequences as the CPU sponge. Both use the same Poseidon2 permutation (verified: constants in poseidon2.cuh match the CPU implementation), the same state layout (WIDTH=16, RATE=8), and the same duplex mode (overwrite-absorb). A verification test should compare GPU-produced challenges against CPU-produced challenges for the same input.","+1. **Transcript determinism**: The GPU Poseidon2 sponge must produce identical results to the CPU sponge. This is verified by the debug_assert_eq on replayed challenges (Change 6). The sponge functions in `sponge.cu` use the same constants and algorithm as `poseidon2.cuh`, which matches the CPU implementation."," ","-2. **No protocol change**: The cryptographic protocol is unchanged. The same challenges are produced in the same order. Only the execution location (CPU → GPU) changes.","+2. **No protocol change**: Same challenges produced in same order. Only execution location changes."," ","-3. **Correctness of eq_r_acc and prev_s_eval**: These accumulators are used by subsequent rounds. The GPU arithmetic must exactly match the CPU EF arithmetic. BabyBear field arithmetic is deterministic (modular arithmetic mod p = 2^27 * 15 + 1).","+3. **FoldEval-only scope**: PrecomputeM rounds use the existing CPU path (with sync overhead). This ensures the optimization is safe for the initial prototype. The PrecomputeM path accounts for a minority of inner rounds (typically the last few per outer round)."," ","-4. **No APC 0 regression**: At APC 0 (99 AIRs, 5 segments), the GKR has fewer rounds per segment. The overhead from initializing device buffers and the final D2H must not exceed the savings. The optimization should be neutral-to-positive at APC 0 since there are still per-round roundtrips to eliminate.","+4. **Sponge state consistency**: After the GKR round loop, the CPU transcript is synchronized via replay (Change 6), not via sync_d2h. The replay produces the exact same state because the transcript is deterministic (same inputs → same outputs)."," ","-5. **Sponge state consistency**: After the GKR round loop, the CPU transcript must be updated with the GPU's final sponge state before ANY subsequent transcript operations. This is critical for Fiat-Shamir security.","+5. **No APC 0 regression**: At APC 0, fewer GKR rounds means less overhead to eliminate. The fixed cost of buffer allocation and post-loop replay is small (~0.1ms). The optimization should be neutral-to-positive."," "," ## Measurement Plan"," ","-1. Build and run the standard benchmarks:","+Build and run the standard benchmarks:"," ```bash"," cd /home/georg/powdr/results/pairing"," cargo build --bin powdr_openvm_riscv -r --features \"metrics,cuda\"","-PROVE_BIN=<path>/release/powdr_openvm_riscv","+PROVE_BIN=$(cargo metadata --format-version 1 --no-deps 2>/dev/null | python3 -c 'import sys,json; print(json.load(sys.stdin)[\"target_directory\"])')/release/powdr_openvm_riscv"," $PROVE_BIN prove --artifact apc300.cbor --input 0 --metrics <path>/after_apc300.json --recursion"," $PROVE_BIN prove --artifact apc000.cbor --input 0 --metrics <path>/after_apc000.json --recursion"," ```"," ","-2. Analyze with `python3 /home/georg/spec.py <metrics_path> <name>`","+Analyze with `python3 /home/georg/spec.py <metrics_path> <name>`."," ","-3. Run nsight profiling for APC 300 to verify inter-kernel gap reduction:","+Run nsight profiling to verify inter-kernel gap reduction:"," ```bash"," nsys profile --output <output> --force-overwrite true --trace cuda,nvtx,osrt --sample none --stats true -- $PROVE_BIN prove --artifact apc300.cbor --input 0 --recursion"," ```"," ","-4. Verify: query nsight sqlite for fractional sumcheck kernel gaps (same analysis as pre-optimization baseline).","-"," ### Expected results","-- LogUp GKR at APC 300: 526ms → ~498ms (28ms reduction from eliminated roundtrips)","-- STARK excl trace at APC 300: 1296ms → ~1268ms","-- Fractional sumcheck inter-kernel gaps: 14ms → ~2ms per segment (mostly kernel launch overhead remaining)","+- LogUp GKR at APC 300: 526ms → ~500ms (FoldEval rounds only; ~26ms from FoldEval gaps eliminated, PrecomputeM gaps remain)","+- STARK excl trace at APC 300: 1296ms → ~1270ms","+- Fractional sumcheck FoldEval inter-kernel gaps: 14ms → ~3ms per segment (residual from kernel launch overhead + PrecomputeM fallback gaps)"," - No regression at APC 0","+- All debug_assert_eq on replayed challenges pass"," "," ### Verification","-- Compare GPU-produced challenges against a reference CPU run for the same proof. All challenges must match exactly.","-- Proof verification must pass at APC 0, 100, and 300.","+- Proof verification must pass at APC 0, 100, and 300","+- Debug assertions on GPU-vs-CPU challenge comparison must hold"," "," ## Rollback Criteria"," "]},{"oldStart":243,"oldLines":4,"newStart":280,"newLines":4,"lines":[" - LogUp GKR improvement at APC 300 is less than 10ms"," - Any regression at APC 0 exceeds 10ms on STARK excl trace"," - Proof verification fails at any APC configuration","-- GPU-produced challenges differ from CPU reference (indicates sponge mismatch)","+- GPU-produced challenges differ from CPU reference (debug_assert fires)"]}],"originalFile":"# Plan: GPU-Side GKR Transcript Processing\n\n## Goal\n\nEliminate per-round CPU-GPU roundtrips in the GKR fractional sumcheck by moving the `observe_and_update` function (D2H copy + arithmetic + Poseidon2 transcript observe/sample) to a GPU kernel. This targets the 14ms per-segment inter-kernel gap measured by nsight in the fractional sumcheck round loop (28ms total across 2 segments at APC 300).\n\n## Current Code Path\n\n### Call chain\n\n```\nfractional_sumcheck_gpu() @ fractional.rs:491\n└─ for round in 1..total_rounds (line 655)\n    ├─ eq_buffer = SqrtEqLayers::from_xi() (line 663) — CPU\n    ├─ lambda = transcript.sample_ext() (line 670) — CPU Poseidon2 sponge\n    ├─ do_sumcheck_round_and_revert() (line 698) — GPU kernel + observe_and_update\n    └─ match backend:\n         ├─ FoldEval: for xi_j { do_fused_sumcheck_round() } — GPU + observe_and_update per inner round\n         └─ PrecomputeM: precompute_m → multifold → observe_and_update (fewer calls)\n```\n\n### Per-round observe_and_update (fractional.rs:318-346)\n\nEach call does:\n1. **D2H sync**: `d_sum_evals.to_host()` — copies 2 EF (32 bytes) from GPU, BLOCKS until preceding GPU kernel completes\n2. **CPU arithmetic**: `reconstruct_s_evals()` — ~12 EF field operations (inverse, interpolation)\n3. **CPU transcript**: `transcript.observe_ext()` × 3 — absorbs 12 BabyBear elements, triggers 1-2 Poseidon2 permutations\n4. **CPU transcript**: `transcript.sample_ext()` — squeezes 4 BabyBear elements, triggers 0-1 Poseidon2 permutations\n5. **CPU accumulator update**: `eq_r_acc *= ...`, `prev_s_eval = ...`\n6. **Return**: challenge `r` used by next round's GPU kernel (passed as scalar argument)\n\n### Why it's slow\n\nnsight profiling of the fractional sumcheck kernel timeline shows:\n- Segment 0: 80.5ms wall time, 14.2ms of inter-kernel gaps across ~41 major kernel transitions (mean gap: 0.35ms)\n- Segment 1: 80.3ms wall time, 14.0ms of inter-kernel gaps\n\nEach gap is a CPU-GPU roundtrip: the GPU idles while the CPU does D2H + reconstruct + transcript + launch next kernel. The GPU compute kernels themselves (compute_round_and_revert, compute_round_and_fold_inplace, multifold, precompute_m) total only ~119ms GPU time per segment.\n\n### Existing GPU infrastructure\n\nThe codebase already has:\n- **GPU Poseidon2 permutation**: `poseidon2_mix(Fp *cells)` in `crates/cuda-common/include/poseidon2.cuh:184-202`\n- **GPU sponge state**: `DeviceSpongeState { Fp state[16]; uint32_t absorb_idx; uint32_t sample_idx; }` in `poseidon2.cuh`\n- **GPU sponge operations**: `sponge_observe(state, val)` and `sponge_sample(state)` device functions in `poseidon2.cuh:24-42`\n- **Host-device sponge sync**: `DuplexSpongeGpu` in `crates/cuda-backend/src/sponge.rs` already synchronizes sponge state between host and device (used by `grind_gpu`)\n\n## Changes\n\n### Change 1: New CUDA kernel `gkr_round_postprocess`\n\n**File**: `crates/cuda-backend/cuda/src/fractional_sumcheck.cu` (new file, or append to existing sumcheck.cu)\n\nCreate a single-thread GPU kernel that replaces `observe_and_update`:\n\n```cuda\n__global__ void gkr_round_postprocess(\n    const FpExt *d_sum_evals,      // 2 EF values from compute_round kernel\n    DeviceSpongeState *sponge,     // transcript sponge state (modified in-place)\n    FpExt *d_prev_s_eval,          // scalar, modified in-place\n    FpExt *d_eq_r_acc,             // scalar, modified in-place\n    FpExt xi_j,                    // constant per call\n    FpExt *d_challenge_out,        // output: sampled challenge r\n    FpExt *d_s_evals_out,          // output: s_evals[0..3] for round_polys_eval\n    FpExt *d_sp_evals_out          // output: sp_evals for debugging/verification (optional)\n) {\n    // 1. Reconstruct s_evals (same logic as fractional.rs:1126-1163)\n    FpExt eq_r_acc = *d_eq_r_acc;\n    FpExt prev_s_eval = *d_prev_s_eval;\n    \n    FpExt sp_evals[3];\n    sp_evals[1] = d_sum_evals[0] * eq_r_acc;\n    sp_evals[2] = d_sum_evals[1] * eq_r_acc;\n    FpExt eq_xi_0 = FpExt::one() - xi_j;\n    FpExt eq_xi_1 = xi_j;\n    sp_evals[0] = (prev_s_eval - eq_xi_1 * sp_evals[1]) * inverse(eq_xi_0);\n    \n    // Interpolate at X=3 (quadratic through 0,1,2)\n    // sp(3) = sp(0) - 3*sp(1) + 3*sp(2) (Newton form for quadratic)\n    // Actually: Lagrange at points 0,1,2 evaluated at 3\n    \n    // Compute s_evals[i] = eq(xi_j, i+1) * sp(i+1)\n    FpExt s_evals[3];\n    for (int i = 0; i < 3; i++) {\n        FpExt x = FpExt::from_u32(i + 1);\n        FpExt sp_x = (i < 2) ? sp_evals[i + 1] : interpolate_quadratic_012(sp_evals, x);\n        s_evals[i] = eval_eq_mle_1(xi_j, x) * sp_x;\n    }\n    d_s_evals_out[0] = s_evals[0];\n    d_s_evals_out[1] = s_evals[1];\n    d_s_evals_out[2] = s_evals[2];\n    \n    // 2. Transcript observe: 3 EF = 12 F\n    for (int i = 0; i < 3; i++) {\n        // observe_ext: absorb 4 base field coefficients\n        for (int c = 0; c < 4; c++) {\n            sponge_observe(*sponge, s_evals[i].coeffs[c]);\n        }\n    }\n    \n    // 3. Transcript sample: 1 EF = 4 F\n    FpExt r;\n    for (int c = 0; c < 4; c++) {\n        r.coeffs[c] = sponge_sample(*sponge);\n    }\n    *d_challenge_out = r;\n    \n    // 4. Update accumulators\n    FpExt eq_r = eval_eq_mle_1(xi_j, r);\n    *d_eq_r_acc = eq_r_acc * eq_r;\n    *d_prev_s_eval = eq_r * interpolate_quadratic_012(sp_evals, r);\n}\n```\n\nThis kernel runs on 1 thread (<<<1,1>>>). It needs:\n- `FpExt` (BinomialExtensionField<BabyBear, 4>) arithmetic in CUDA: add, sub, mul, inverse\n- `eval_eq_mle_1(xi, x)` = `xi * x + (1-xi) * (1-x)` — trivial\n- `interpolate_quadratic_012(vals, x)` — Lagrange interpolation of degree-2 poly through points (0,1,2) evaluated at x\n- Existing `sponge_observe` and `sponge_sample` from poseidon2.cuh\n\n### Change 2: EF arithmetic in CUDA\n\n**File**: `crates/cuda-common/include/ext_field.cuh` (new or extend existing)\n\nIf EF (BinomialExtensionField<BabyBear,4>) arithmetic doesn't already exist in CUDA device code, implement:\n- `FpExt` struct: 4 `Fp` coefficients\n- Add, sub, mul (using the binomial extension multiplication formula)\n- Inverse (using extended Euclidean or Fermat's little theorem)\n- `from_u32`, `one()`, `zero()`\n\nCheck first: the GKR input evaluation kernel already uses `FracExt` which contains `FpExt` members. The extension field arithmetic likely exists in the codebase.\n\n### Change 3: FFI wrapper for gkr_round_postprocess\n\n**File**: `crates/cuda-backend/src/cuda/fractional.rs` (new, or extend existing cuda module)\n\nAdd Rust FFI binding:\n```rust\nextern \"C\" {\n    fn _gkr_round_postprocess(\n        d_sum_evals: *const EF,\n        sponge: *mut DeviceSpongeState,\n        d_prev_s_eval: *mut EF,\n        d_eq_r_acc: *mut EF,\n        xi_j: EF,  // passed by value (16 bytes)\n        d_challenge_out: *mut EF,\n        d_s_evals_out: *mut EF,\n        d_sp_evals_out: *mut EF,\n    ) -> i32;\n}\n```\n\n### Change 4: Modify fractional sumcheck round loop\n\n**File**: `crates/cuda-backend/src/logup_zerocheck/fractional.rs`\n\nBefore the GKR round loop (line 655):\n1. **Upload sponge state**: Sync the current CPU transcript state to a `DeviceBuffer<DeviceSpongeState>` (the existing `DuplexSpongeGpu::sync_h2d()` pattern)\n2. **Allocate device buffers**: `d_prev_s_eval`, `d_eq_r_acc`, `d_challenges` (array of total_rounds EF), `d_round_polys_eval` (array of total_rounds × 3 EF), `d_sp_evals_scratch`\n\nReplace `observe_and_update()` calls (lines 382, and within do_fused_sumcheck_round and PrecomputeM paths) with:\n```rust\nunsafe { gkr_round_postprocess(\n    d_sum_evals.as_ptr(),\n    d_sponge.as_mut_ptr(),\n    d_prev_s_eval.as_mut_ptr(),\n    d_eq_r_acc.as_mut_ptr(),\n    xi_j,\n    d_challenges.as_mut_ptr().add(challenge_idx),\n    d_round_polys_ptr.add(round_polys_idx * 3),\n    d_sp_evals_scratch.as_mut_ptr(),\n)?; }\n```\n\nModify the next round's compute kernel to read the challenge from `d_challenges[challenge_idx]` instead of receiving it as a scalar argument. The challenge is already on device — pass a device pointer.\n\n### Change 5: Post-loop D2H and CPU state synchronization\n\n**File**: `crates/cuda-backend/src/logup_zerocheck/fractional.rs`\n\nAfter the GKR round loop completes:\n1. **D2H**: Copy `d_round_polys_eval`, `d_challenges` (r_vec), `d_eq_r_acc`, `d_prev_s_eval`, `d_sponge` back to host\n2. **Update CPU transcript**: Restore the CPU transcript's sponge state from the downloaded GPU sponge state (reverse of sync_h2d)\n3. **Populate CPU data structures**: Fill `round_polys_eval` and `r_vec` from the D2H'd arrays\n4. **Continue**: The rest of the proof uses the CPU transcript as before\n\n### Change 6: Pass challenge by device pointer to compute_round kernels\n\n**Files**: CUDA kernel wrappers in `crates/cuda-backend/src/cuda/logup_zerocheck.rs`, CUDA kernels in `crates/cuda-backend/cuda/src/sumcheck.cu`\n\nModify `frac_compute_round_and_fold`, `frac_compute_round_and_revert`, etc. to accept the previous round's challenge `r` via device pointer instead of scalar value. Add a `__device__` read at kernel start:\n```cuda\nFpExt r = *d_r_ptr;  // read from device memory (16 bytes, L1 cached)\n```\n\nThis is a minimal change to the existing kernels. The pointer argument replaces the scalar argument. Read latency is negligible (L1 cache hit).\n\n## Invariants\n\n1. **Transcript determinism**: The GPU sponge must produce identical observe/sample sequences as the CPU sponge. Both use the same Poseidon2 permutation (verified: constants in poseidon2.cuh match the CPU implementation), the same state layout (WIDTH=16, RATE=8), and the same duplex mode (overwrite-absorb). A verification test should compare GPU-produced challenges against CPU-produced challenges for the same input.\n\n2. **No protocol change**: The cryptographic protocol is unchanged. The same challenges are produced in the same order. Only the execution location (CPU → GPU) changes.\n\n3. **Correctness of eq_r_acc and prev_s_eval**: These accumulators are used by subsequent rounds. The GPU arithmetic must exactly match the CPU EF arithmetic. BabyBear field arithmetic is deterministic (modular arithmetic mod p = 2^27 * 15 + 1).\n\n4. **No APC 0 regression**: At APC 0 (99 AIRs, 5 segments), the GKR has fewer rounds per segment. The overhead from initializing device buffers and the final D2H must not exceed the savings. The optimization should be neutral-to-positive at APC 0 since there are still per-round roundtrips to eliminate.\n\n5. **Sponge state consistency**: After the GKR round loop, the CPU transcript must be updated with the GPU's final sponge state before ANY subsequent transcript operations. This is critical for Fiat-Shamir security.\n\n## Measurement Plan\n\n1. Build and run the standard benchmarks:\n```bash\ncd /home/georg/powdr/results/pairing\ncargo build --bin powdr_openvm_riscv -r --features \"metrics,cuda\"\nPROVE_BIN=<path>/release/powdr_openvm_riscv\n$PROVE_BIN prove --artifact apc300.cbor --input 0 --metrics <path>/after_apc300.json --recursion\n$PROVE_BIN prove --artifact apc000.cbor --input 0 --metrics <path>/after_apc000.json --recursion\n```\n\n2. Analyze with `python3 /home/georg/spec.py <metrics_path> <name>`\n\n3. Run nsight profiling for APC 300 to verify inter-kernel gap reduction:\n```bash\nnsys profile --output <output> --force-overwrite true --trace cuda,nvtx,osrt --sample none --stats true -- $PROVE_BIN prove --artifact apc300.cbor --input 0 --recursion\n```\n\n4. Verify: query nsight sqlite for fractional sumcheck kernel gaps (same analysis as pre-optimization baseline).\n\n### Expected results\n- LogUp GKR at APC 300: 526ms → ~498ms (28ms reduction from eliminated roundtrips)\n- STARK excl trace at APC 300: 1296ms → ~1268ms\n- Fractional sumcheck inter-kernel gaps: 14ms → ~2ms per segment (mostly kernel launch overhead remaining)\n- No regression at APC 0\n\n### Verification\n- Compare GPU-produced challenges against a reference CPU run for the same proof. All challenges must match exactly.\n- Proof verification must pass at APC 0, 100, and 300.\n\n## Rollback Criteria\n\nRevert if:\n- LogUp GKR improvement at APC 300 is less than 10ms\n- Any regression at APC 0 exceeds 10ms on STARK excl trace\n- Proof verification fails at any APC configuration\n- GPU-produced challenges differ from CPU reference (indicates sponge mismatch)\n"}}
