{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_0132xQJWsCgrSmx8K1aNtKkW","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# 2026-04-14-1800-gpu-gkr-transcript-processing\n2\t\n3\tEliminate per-round CPU-GPU roundtrips in the GKR fractional sumcheck by moving the transcript observe/sample operations to GPU. Currently, each GKR sumcheck round launches a GPU kernel, then does a blocking D2H copy of 2 field elements, CPU-side arithmetic (reconstruct_s_evals), CPU-side Poseidon2 transcript absorb/squeeze, and finally launches the next round's kernel. With ~20 rounds per segment × 2 segments = ~40 roundtrips, nsight profiling shows 14ms of inter-kernel gap per segment (28ms total) from these CPU-GPU transitions. By implementing a small GPU kernel that fuses the post-round processing (reconstruct, sponge observe, sponge squeeze, accumulator update), we eliminate all mid-loop D2H copies and keep the entire GKR round loop on the GPU, with a single D2H at the end to retrieve the final transcript state.\n4\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01RAfV4P8puVUmVVW47UfM8T","session_id":"e33616ae-361c-4ad4-bcb1-e94f2f710e44","uuid":"c6de54e7-4eff-4b41-ba6e-d1ea55f3e3be","timestamp":"2026-04-14T16:35:51.748Z"}
